WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:10.480
Today, we'll continue
these geometric type--

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:13.240
describing these
geometric type problems.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:16.625
One thing that is going
to come up-- actually,

00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:18.250
maybe in a couple of
weeks in the class

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.020
is the concept of
an analytic center.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:23.260
It's actually not an
analytic center of a set,

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:27.695
although people informally say
the analytic center of a set.

00:00:27.695 --> 00:00:28.570
They don't mean that.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:32.620
What they really mean is the
analytic center of a set of--

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:36.280
of a description of a set,
which is a set of inequalities.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:40.460
And the analytic
center goes like this.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.220
So let's say we have--

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.650
I mean, the equality constraints
we just keep right here.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.220
But suppose we have a
bunch of inequalities here.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:51.100
And what this means--

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.010
so obviously, the fi of
x should be less than 0.

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:58.630
Minus fi of x is what
people would call the margin

00:00:58.630 --> 00:00:59.590
when that's positive.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:01.720
So minus fi of x is the margin.

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.349
And it's roughly how much
do you have left before you

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:06.530
violate the inequality.

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:07.600
That's the margin.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.730
And the so-called analytic
center of a set of inequalities

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:20.395
is given by minimizing
the negative log of the--

00:01:20.395 --> 00:01:22.750
this is the margin,
minus fi of x.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:25.210
Another way to say
it is you maximize

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.225
the product of the margins.

00:01:29.225 --> 00:01:30.850
This comes up in a
lot of other things,

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:33.760
in log utilities it's used
universally, networking

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:34.700
and things like that.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:38.590
So this is not at all uncommon.

00:01:38.590 --> 00:01:40.730
So that is the so-called
analytic center.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.840
Oh, of course that's
a convex problem.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:43.340
Why?

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:50.180
Because minus log minus argument
is convex and increasing.

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:54.920
And then the fi's
of x are Convex

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:57.200
And we'll actually
soon we'll see

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.300
very soon that this
is actually quite

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.580
straightforward to compute.

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.755
Matter of fact, you will
actually be doing-- well,

00:02:03.755 --> 00:02:06.710
you'll be writing code
that will basically

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:10.100
compute analytic centers
and things like that.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.750
So that's the analytic--

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:15.025
that is the analytic center
of a set of inequalities.

00:02:15.025 --> 00:02:16.400
And here's just
a picture of what

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.770
it looks like for a polyhedron.

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:22.790
So this shows you
what's feasible.

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.750
Each of these corresponds
to one of the rows here,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.970
each of those lines.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.090
The dashed curves give
you the level curves

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.400
of this log barrier.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:35.720
This is called the log barrier.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.100
It's called a log
barrier, I think

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.058
that's maybe borrowed
from physics or something

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:42.400
like that because it's a
potential function that goes up

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.250
to infinity as you
approach the boundary.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:49.420
So that's a barrier for a
particle or something like that

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.680
if that were a-- if
that were an energy.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.080
And a couple of
things you can see.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:57.820
It's actually cool.

00:02:57.820 --> 00:02:59.920
So we've marked there
the analytic center.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.240
That's the point where
that barrier is minimized.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:05.320
So whatever that
point is, it maximizes

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:10.510
the product actually of the
margins for the inequalities.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.010
So that's what it does.

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:18.730
And you can see actually that
the level sets close down

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.170
to the analytic
center are, of course,

00:03:21.170 --> 00:03:23.260
going to be very
close to ellipsoidal.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:29.560
And that's of course, true
for any smooth function.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:36.350
So the dark ellipsoid
drawn there is this.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.590
It turns out that for
the analytic center,

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.990
if you calculate
the Hessian of it,

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:44.030
then the ellipsoid defined
by the center but with a 1

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.550
on the right hand
side, that ellipsoid

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.245
is guaranteed to
be inside the set.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.510
This will be important later.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:59.640
If you puff that ellipsoid up
by a factor around m-- actually,

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.290
m would be enough
because that would be

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.740
square root of m squared there.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:07.080
If you puff it up
by a factor of m,

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:08.430
then you cover the ellipsoid.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.090
So basically it says that
the ellipsoids defined

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:16.050
by analytic centers also have
this approximation property.

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.600
That it gives you
an inside ellipsoid.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.890
And then when you puff it up
by a factor, it covers the set.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.080
So it gives you
this approximation.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.800
Here, the
approximation depends--

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.650
now remember that for things
like the Lowner-John ellipsoid,

00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:33.840
or the minimum volume
covering ellipsoid,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.390
or the maximum volume
inscribed ellipsoid,

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:38.310
there it depended
only on the dimension.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:41.310
Here, it depends on m which
is the number of inequalities

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:42.950
here.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:46.580
So if m is much larger than
n, this is a worse bound.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:50.960
Worse approximation
bound of a convex set.

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:55.425
But anyway, you'll be
seeing much more about this

00:04:55.425 --> 00:04:56.300
in a couple of weeks.

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:03.080
What we're going to look at
now is linear discrimination.

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.820
Probably most of you
have seen this maybe

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.600
in some basic machine
learning class.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:09.950
But here's the problem.

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:13.160
And there's many, many
variations on this.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.220
But it looks like this.

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:17.030
I'm going to give you a set
of points x1 through xn.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:18.020
These are vectors.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:19.465
And a bunch of ys.

00:05:19.465 --> 00:05:20.840
And if you like,
these are simply

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:24.110
feature vectors associated
with a positive outcome

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:28.100
and a negative outcome in
some kind of Boolean problem.

00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:29.840
So I have some data.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.550
And what we seek
is a hyperplane.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:38.675
So that's defined by a
vector a and a constant b.

00:05:38.675 --> 00:05:43.340
And we want all the xis to be
on one side of that hyperplane

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:45.360
and we want all the ys
to be on the other side.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:51.140
So that's what we want,
separating hyperplane.

00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:54.890
Shockingly, this was
studied in the early 1950s.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:56.390
People came up with
something called

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:59.370
the perceptron algorithm which
you might have seen somewhere.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.110
It's a very early thing.

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:05.580
And weirdly, many years
went by before someone

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:09.600
looked up and said,
dude, that's an LP.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.150
In which case, people
literally down the hall

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:13.950
know how to solve these.

00:06:13.950 --> 00:06:15.870
So it's just one of
those cases where

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.380
the diffusion of knowledge,
it took some time.

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:23.160
So there is a subtlety here
that we're going to look at.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.230
Oh, one thing we have to be
careful of is the notation.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:32.496
So here, the variables
are basically a and b.

00:06:32.496 --> 00:06:35.460
And the data are
the xis and the yis,

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:38.490
which violates this usual
convention that things

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:40.550
at the end of the
alphabet are typically

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:42.900
are variables and things at
the beginning are typically

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:45.570
your parameters or data.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.830
So you have to be
on your toes here.

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:51.960
So what sort of inequality
on a and b is this?

00:06:57.420 --> 00:07:02.826
What kind of function is
a transpose xi plus b?

00:07:02.826 --> 00:07:07.020
What kind of function
of the pair ab is that?

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:08.310
It's linear.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.350
OK, that's linear.

00:07:10.350 --> 00:07:14.490
Now there is one thing here, we
have these strict inequalities.

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:18.400
So let me just say a
little bit about that.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.780
There are a lot of cases where
strict inequalities are silly,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:24.300
and just mean nothing, and
you just quietly replace them

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:26.370
with inequalities.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.090
So here would be an example.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.870
You're working with someone
and they're designing a power

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:33.390
amplifier and they
go, your budget

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:36.090
is 50 milliwatts in your design.

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.438
And you go, cool, OK.

00:07:37.438 --> 00:07:39.480
And then they come back
and they go, it's strict.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:41.700
You have to be less
than 50 milliwatts.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:42.990
Not less than or equal to.

00:07:42.990 --> 00:07:45.748
And it's like, OK, this
is just completely idiotic

00:07:45.748 --> 00:07:46.540
and makes no sense.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.790
Everybody see what I'm saying?

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:52.530
So basically, quietly you try
to explain to the person that

00:07:52.530 --> 00:07:53.680
doesn't make any sense.

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.710
And you quietly replace
less strictly less than

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:58.710
with less than or equal
to and no one gets hurt.

00:07:58.710 --> 00:08:00.060
Everyone got this?

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.830
OK, so that's fine.

00:08:01.830 --> 00:08:07.210
Now if we try that here,
let's see what happens.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:11.440
So you'd say, well, ai
transpose x plus b is

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.730
bigger than or equal to 0.

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:16.960
And I also want a transpose yi
plus b less than or equal to 0.

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:18.790
Anybody got any
suggestions about how

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.830
to solve that system
of inequalities?

00:08:23.450 --> 00:08:24.760
a and b 0.

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:25.720
A and b 0.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:26.380
I like it.

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:28.360
So a and b 0 works.

00:08:28.360 --> 00:08:30.880
So this is one of
those cases where

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:33.820
the distinction between
strict and non-strict

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.309
actually matters.

00:08:36.309 --> 00:08:39.015
It actually matters because
when you make it non-strict--

00:08:39.015 --> 00:08:40.390
when you made it
non-strict, it's

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:44.830
got a silly solution
which is uninteresting.

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:48.440
So in a lot of these cases,
you actually have to hand,

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:49.660
argue each one.

00:08:49.660 --> 00:08:52.900
I'm not aware of a
general principle.

00:08:52.900 --> 00:08:57.580
Hand argue each one and I'll
do that argument right now.

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:01.210
The main arguments go
something like this.

00:09:01.210 --> 00:09:04.900
That inequality is homogeneous.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:08.710
In other words, if I double a
and b, then whatever this is,

00:09:08.710 --> 00:09:10.120
which is supposed
to be positive,

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.040
goes up by a factor 2.

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:12.850
Everybody agree?

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.280
Same over here for this one.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:16.390
Same for the other one.

00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:19.300
If this is supposed
to be less than--

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:21.530
if I double a and b,
these things are negative.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:22.280
Whatever are they?

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:23.660
They just got twice as negative.

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:25.250
Everybody following this?

00:09:25.250 --> 00:09:28.370
So what I could
do is my claim is

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:30.890
that I can without
loss of generality

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:33.320
replace these
strict inequalities

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.085
with these non-strict
inequalities with a 1.

00:09:36.085 --> 00:09:37.910
And the way I do
that is up here,

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:41.480
if you give me an a and a b
that satisfies this, I simply

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:45.300
scale them by whatever
factor is needed to make--

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.300
however positive that was
how negative that was,

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:49.908
I multiply by a
factor which makes

00:09:49.908 --> 00:09:51.200
that bigger than or equal to 1.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:51.980
Everybody got it?

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:54.950
So that says if there
exists a and b that satisfy

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:58.820
that, then by multiplying
them by a positive number,

00:09:58.820 --> 00:10:00.590
they're going to satisfy this.

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:02.630
The converse goes like this.

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.610
Converse is relatively
straightforward.

00:10:04.610 --> 00:10:07.670
Because if I tell you a number
is bigger than or equal to 1,

00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:10.340
it's definitely positive.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:11.913
So everyone got this?

00:10:11.913 --> 00:10:14.330
There's actually a subtlety,
it's actually quite important

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:16.460
in I don't know.

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:20.215
You could easily encounter this.

00:10:20.215 --> 00:10:23.770
I mean, actually both in
research or other things,

00:10:23.770 --> 00:10:24.890
or other places.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:28.780
So this is something we
expect you to know about.

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:30.010
Yeah?

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:33.190
Is there anything stopping
us from saying a transpose x

00:10:33.190 --> 00:10:34.960
plus b greater than a million?

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.460
No, it's fine.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:39.130
That's totally cool.

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:43.060
So if you want to put 10 to the
6 here and minus 10 to the 6,

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:45.430
that's perfectly good.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:48.070
And then to be quite
precise, what I'm saying then

00:10:48.070 --> 00:10:52.990
is the first set of
inequalities are feasible if

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.120
and only if these
are feasible, if

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:59.710
and only if your variation
where one is replaced

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:01.780
with 10 to the 6 is feasible.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:07.930
And if one is feasible, all
of them are feasible, period.

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:10.780
And if one is infeasible,
all are infeasible.

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:13.030
And how do we get-- how do
we translate among them?

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:15.370
We just scale them
by various things.

00:11:15.370 --> 00:11:17.340
So if I have an a and
b that satisfies these

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:19.840
and I want one that satisfies
yours, I just multiply a and b

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:23.170
by a million, and then
they'll satisfy it.

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:24.070
Make sense?

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:28.360
All right.

00:11:28.360 --> 00:11:32.980
So we did it very quietly, or
I did it with just half a line

00:11:32.980 --> 00:11:34.450
saying they're
homogeneous in ab.

00:11:34.450 --> 00:11:36.580
But I just gave you
the full argument

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:37.585
for why that's the case.

00:11:40.960 --> 00:11:42.470
So what does this say?

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:43.600
Oh, sorry, we can go back.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:47.515
This says that if you
gave me a pile of vectors,

00:11:47.515 --> 00:11:51.820
one set of vectors and another
set and you wanted to know

00:11:51.820 --> 00:11:53.890
can separate them
with a hyperplane,

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:57.250
and the answer is just it's
an LP feasibility problem.

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:58.100
That's what it says.

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:01.870
So it means that that's
immediately tractable.

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:03.085
We could just solve this.

00:12:06.050 --> 00:12:08.100
Well, we can do more.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:11.450
You can do things like this.

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:15.020
You can actually say, well, now
that I know that there is one,

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:18.200
I might want to pick one of
these separating hyperplanes.

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:22.610
And one way you might do
it is something like this.

00:12:22.610 --> 00:12:26.480
If I have two
hyperplanes like that

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:30.830
and they're normalized with
a 1 on the right hand side,

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:34.130
or minus 1, the
distance between them

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:38.750
is 2 divided by the 2 norm of
a, you can just calculate that.

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:40.820
In fact, this is a slab.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:46.220
So this middle thing, this is a
transpose x plus b equals zero.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:48.030
This is, I know it
doesn't really matter,

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:50.990
but this is either, let's say,
the minus 1 and this is plus 1,

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:52.102
let's say.

00:12:52.102 --> 00:12:53.060
So that's what that is.

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:58.910
And the width of that slab is
going to be 2 over the square--

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:01.430
2 over the 2 norm of a.

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:04.100
By the way, what kind
of function is this?

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:05.235
Convex, concave?

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:13.690
It's a trick question but a
perfectly good one, I think.

00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:16.630
What kind of function
is that of a?

00:13:23.534 --> 00:13:25.813
The answer is, it's
neither convex or concave.

00:13:25.813 --> 00:13:26.980
I was just messing with you.

00:13:26.980 --> 00:13:29.170
I just wanted to see if
you'd-- anyway, it's fine.

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:30.760
It's not.

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:36.910
So it's Nothing So if
you're saying maximize this,

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:38.425
it's not concave,
that's for sure.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:44.890
You can picture it in your
mind if you want, right,

00:13:44.890 --> 00:13:48.220
in R2, the norm.

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:49.850
It's got level curves like this.

00:13:49.850 --> 00:13:51.860
And that thing is a function.

00:13:51.860 --> 00:13:54.550
It's a crazy thing with
a weird pole, it's 0.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:55.550
It goes up to infinity.

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:58.720
So it's not convex
and it's not concave.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:14:00.460
Actually, in some
directions it's convex

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:01.630
and in others it's concave.

00:14:04.570 --> 00:14:06.790
Doesn't mean we
can't maximize it.

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:10.810
Because maximizing this is the
same as minimizing 1 over it

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:13.420
because it's positive.

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:16.720
So that says this
problem is convex,

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:19.060
that's a quadratic program.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:23.930
And if you solve this
problem, this QP,

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:28.070
you'll actually
get the thickest--

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:32.540
you'll get the thickest
separating slab.

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:34.220
Makes sense?

00:14:34.220 --> 00:14:36.140
And in fact, we can
even say a little bit

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:39.170
how there's an interesting
way to get that slab.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:41.810
So for this problem
over here, I think

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:44.540
this shows you the separating
slab for these points

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:46.460
and these points.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:47.675
All make sense?

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:53.490
And let's see, that's a QP.

00:14:53.490 --> 00:14:54.990
Again, you have
to be very careful

00:14:54.990 --> 00:14:56.907
because you have to see
what the variables are

00:14:56.907 --> 00:14:58.320
and all that sort of stuff.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:03.420
Here the variables are a
and b, xi and yi are data.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:07.263
So that's robust
linear discrimination

00:15:07.263 --> 00:15:08.430
is the way you might say it.

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:09.450
That's dialect.

00:15:09.450 --> 00:15:12.645
That's old school
machine learning dialect.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:18.090
Now, it's actually kind
of interesting to take

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:24.600
for this problem to work out
what is the dual of this QP.

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:25.560
It's not a QP.

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:27.100
Sorry, I misspoke.

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:29.025
It's not a QP unless
you square it.

00:15:29.025 --> 00:15:31.890
And that's an equivalent
problem and that's a QP.

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:34.590
This would be, I guess, an SOCP.

00:15:34.590 --> 00:15:38.190
But I think that none
of this would matter.

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:44.100
So if you work out what
the Lagrange dual is,

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:46.380
you get something
that looks like this.

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.105
You had these two
sets of inequalities.

00:15:48.105 --> 00:15:50.230
And you end up with something
that looks like this.

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:52.660
You should maximize
1 transpose lambda

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:57.348
plus 1 transpose mu
subject to, and you get

00:15:57.348 --> 00:15:58.640
something that looks like this.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:16:00.097
It's actually interesting.

00:16:00.097 --> 00:16:02.680
And it says that the sum of the
lambdas and the sum of the mus

00:16:02.680 --> 00:16:07.930
have to be equal, and you
get something like that.

00:16:07.930 --> 00:16:10.420
And the optimal value
of that top problem

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:13.600
is actually the maximum
margin of separation.

00:16:16.825 --> 00:16:20.950
It's the norm or whatever it is.

00:16:20.950 --> 00:16:23.150
So if you stare at
this long enough,

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:26.260
you realize that
actually what we can do

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:30.550
is change variables to normalize
the lambdas and the mus.

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:32.440
They're both
non-negative vectors.

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:37.420
And simply, we replace them
with vectors that sum up to 1

00:16:37.420 --> 00:16:40.610
and are non-negative, otherwise
known as mixture coefficients.

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:44.257
So we do that and you
plug that in up here

00:16:44.257 --> 00:16:45.340
and various things happen.

00:16:45.340 --> 00:16:47.530
And you end up with
this problem here.

00:16:47.530 --> 00:16:52.270
And now this is very cool
because now we can look at it.

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:55.270
This says that theta is a
set of mixture coefficients.

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:59.590
And so this is a general
point in the convex hull

00:16:59.590 --> 00:17:04.099
of the original xs, because
it's a mixture of them.

00:17:04.099 --> 00:17:07.540
This thing, here, again,
these are coefficients that

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:09.010
are non-negative, add up to 1.

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:12.579
This thing here is
actually a general point

00:17:12.579 --> 00:17:15.069
in the convex hull of the yis.

00:17:15.069 --> 00:17:19.540
And what this says is you should
minimize t subject to this norm

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:20.200
less than t.

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:22.599
Obviously, it's the same as
just minimizing this thing.

00:17:22.599 --> 00:17:25.460
This is the epigraph
formulation.

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:30.410
And so what it really saying
is very geometrically clear.

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:35.490
It says minimize the
distance between two points.

00:17:35.490 --> 00:17:38.090
One is in the convex
hull of the xs

00:17:38.090 --> 00:17:39.740
and one in the convex
hull of the ys.

00:17:39.740 --> 00:17:41.930
Does that make sense?

00:17:41.930 --> 00:17:43.680
So it's actually very cool.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:47.930
So if we go back here,
the primal, basically it

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:53.720
says expand the
slab until it stops

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:55.310
fitting between the two sets.

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:58.850
The other one says, form the
convex hull of the dark points

00:17:58.850 --> 00:17:59.820
in your head.

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:01.370
Just do that visually.

00:18:01.370 --> 00:18:05.900
Form the convex hull of
the white circles here.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:09.710
And then find the two
points in those two sets

00:18:09.710 --> 00:18:11.270
which are closest to each other.

00:18:11.270 --> 00:18:14.420
And sure enough, you're
going to get something like,

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:17.210
I don't know, probably that
dark point and then a point

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:19.340
over there.

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:21.620
All make sense?

00:18:21.620 --> 00:18:25.500
So it just means
that in these, a lot

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:28.320
of these geometric problems,
duals are interpretable.

00:18:28.320 --> 00:18:31.050
Duals are usually highly
interpretable and interesting.

00:18:31.050 --> 00:18:32.550
I mean doesn't help
you do anything.

00:18:32.550 --> 00:18:34.290
It doesn't solve it
better, blah, blah, blah.

00:18:34.290 --> 00:18:36.873
But it's just interesting just
to see that it all makes sense.

00:18:41.290 --> 00:18:44.750
I think we looked at that.

00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:48.470
And now we get to something
that's actually well useful.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:49.810
So here it is.

00:18:53.320 --> 00:18:57.190
Now what it is you
have points that

00:18:57.190 --> 00:18:59.830
are not linearly separable.

00:18:59.830 --> 00:19:02.908
And again, if you've had a first
course maybe in the first two

00:19:02.908 --> 00:19:05.200
weeks of a machine learning
class, you would have seen.

00:19:05.200 --> 00:19:07.870
This is the so-called--
it's getting

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:10.210
close to the idea of the
support vector machine which

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:11.840
we'll get to in a minute.

00:19:11.840 --> 00:19:13.690
But what you do is
you add-- and this

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:17.200
is in the style of how it's
described in a machine learning

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:19.900
class, which I think is poorly
but that doesn't matter.

00:19:19.900 --> 00:19:21.100
That's fine.

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:23.050
And so what you do is
you say, well, look,

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:24.760
I can't satisfy
these inequalities.

00:19:24.760 --> 00:19:28.870
And you go, cool, I'm going
to give you some slack.

00:19:28.870 --> 00:19:31.990
By the way, that parsed
both mathematically and

00:19:31.990 --> 00:19:33.400
colloquially.

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:36.760
So what it means is instead of
requiring these things to be

00:19:36.760 --> 00:19:39.760
bigger than 1, I'm going to say,
they're bigger than 1 minus ui.

00:19:39.760 --> 00:19:41.740
ui is non-negative.

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:42.580
It's your slack.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:44.600
It's how much we're
going to shrink.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:47.150
We're going to shrink the
requirement on the constraint

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:49.510
so you can be-- so
you can satisfy it.

00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:52.750
And then we're going to say
something like, let's minimize

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:55.120
the sum of these slacks.

00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:57.610
How much do you actually--
how many inequalities

00:19:57.610 --> 00:19:59.860
do you actually need-- do
I need to give you slack

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:01.450
on so you can satisfy them?

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:05.590
And you'd get a problem
that looks like this.

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:08.620
And it's actually quite
interesting here, we

00:20:08.620 --> 00:20:09.430
would do it--

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:14.530
we would write this in a very
simple way but that's OK.

00:20:14.530 --> 00:20:17.080
And it turns out this
is actually useful.

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:20.800
Now it's a heuristic for
minimizing roughly the number

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:25.540
of misclassified points
which I can show you

00:20:25.540 --> 00:20:26.350
what that would be.

00:20:26.350 --> 00:20:28.093
Actually, why don't
I show you that?

00:20:28.093 --> 00:20:29.510
So here's what it
would look like.

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:34.070
Let's see, I will write down.

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:35.030
Let me see what it is.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:41.330
So this is ai transpose--

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:44.480
a transpose xi plus b.

00:20:44.480 --> 00:20:47.780
We want that-- here.

00:20:47.780 --> 00:20:49.580
Here's 0.

00:20:49.580 --> 00:20:53.450
And if I ask you whether you
have misclassified a point xi

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:57.440
or not, the question is
whether this is negative.

00:20:57.440 --> 00:21:01.840
So if you're going
to misclassify,

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:04.190
that's something
that looks like this.

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:05.960
That's a cost.

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:10.310
So if I took this function
of these, actually

00:21:10.310 --> 00:21:14.150
what I would end up getting is
precisely the number of data

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:15.617
points I misclassified.

00:21:15.617 --> 00:21:16.700
Everybody agree with that?

00:21:16.700 --> 00:21:18.158
I mean because if
this is negative,

00:21:18.158 --> 00:21:20.210
it means I wanted this
thing to be positive.

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:23.870
Or here, I can-- here, if
you insist that's positive,

00:21:23.870 --> 00:21:26.810
I guess I have to
make it like that.

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:27.990
So that's what it is.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:30.400
So now this function,
of course, is not convex

00:21:30.400 --> 00:21:33.910
so you can't minimize that.

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:37.300
Then what this says
is you should actually

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:39.010
do something like this.

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:43.770
You should say, I want
that to be at least 1.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:45.560
And then your cost
function-- the cost

00:21:45.560 --> 00:21:51.900
function we have looks like
this because it's the amount.

00:21:51.900 --> 00:21:53.580
And actually, it's
super interesting

00:21:53.580 --> 00:21:54.670
and makes perfect sense.

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:56.940
That's equivalent to
this problem here.

00:21:56.940 --> 00:21:59.910
That's these numbers up
here, the u's and v's.

00:21:59.910 --> 00:22:01.980
So that's convex.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:03.632
And it's actually
super interesting

00:22:03.632 --> 00:22:04.840
to think about what it means.

00:22:04.840 --> 00:22:05.820
Here's what it means.

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:09.300
It says that if you
have a data point--

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:12.480
and we think of 1 as the margin.

00:22:12.480 --> 00:22:14.460
So it says, you know what?

00:22:14.460 --> 00:22:18.120
If you classify
correctly with at least--

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:21.060
over here means you classify
correctly, bigger than 0.

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:25.390
If you classify correctly
with a margin of 1 or more,

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:26.400
then that's cool.

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:28.080
There's no cost.

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:29.790
You did fine.

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:32.460
You start charging over here.

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:37.710
We're actually charging for
classifying correctly but being

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:39.690
less than the margin we desire.

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:42.270
Everybody following this?

00:22:42.270 --> 00:22:45.440
And then it just grows linearly.

00:22:45.440 --> 00:22:47.440
And actually, you can see
something immediately,

00:22:47.440 --> 00:22:50.960
which is this function is
bigger than this other function.

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:53.513
And so it turns out that
the optimal value of this

00:22:53.513 --> 00:22:55.180
is actually an upper
bound on the number

00:22:55.180 --> 00:22:57.160
of misclassifications.

00:22:57.160 --> 00:23:01.000
So again, if you're
following this that's great.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:03.640
And if you aren't,
that's probably OK too.

00:23:06.220 --> 00:23:07.700
All makes sense?

00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:11.230
So how many people
actually have seen things

00:23:11.230 --> 00:23:12.910
like this in a machine
learning class?

00:23:12.910 --> 00:23:14.350
I hope a fair number of people.

00:23:14.350 --> 00:23:14.850
Good.

00:23:17.230 --> 00:23:20.650
And then all you
do now is you add--

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:23.620
I guess, they would actually
square this probably up here.

00:23:23.620 --> 00:23:25.930
They would square this thing.

00:23:25.930 --> 00:23:29.770
And then this trades
off two things.

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:32.780
This will trade off the
two desirable things.

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:35.350
Number one, having small slacks.

00:23:35.350 --> 00:23:39.230
Small slacks corresponds
to this surrogate function.

00:23:39.230 --> 00:23:41.080
In fact, there's a
whole way to view

00:23:41.080 --> 00:23:44.020
this which makes perfect
sense, which is someone says,

00:23:44.020 --> 00:23:46.930
find me a hyperplane
that misclassifies

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:48.100
as few points as possible.

00:23:48.100 --> 00:23:50.300
And you go, cool, the
function looks like this.

00:23:50.300 --> 00:23:53.290
And you go, I can't do that
because it's not convex.

00:23:53.290 --> 00:23:58.840
Then you'd say, well, I'll
use this convex function here.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:00.190
Makes perfect sense, right?

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:01.750
And you say, I'll minimize that.

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:05.110
Everybody following this?

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:09.520
Anyway, by the way often
in many, many fields,

00:24:09.520 --> 00:24:12.550
that story usually has
a very good outcome.

00:24:12.550 --> 00:24:14.980
Someone says, I want to do x.

00:24:14.980 --> 00:24:18.460
And you'd say, we can't
do that, not convex.

00:24:18.460 --> 00:24:22.510
Then someone says, but here's
a surrogate which is convex.

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:25.270
And then people observe it to
work really well in practice.

00:24:25.270 --> 00:24:27.310
Usually about five
years later, somebody

00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:32.170
writes a theory-oriented paper
that basically says, actually,

00:24:32.170 --> 00:24:34.630
you never even wanted to
solve the first thing which

00:24:34.630 --> 00:24:37.120
you can't solve anyway
because it's a bad idea.

00:24:37.120 --> 00:24:38.500
You really wanted to--

00:24:38.500 --> 00:24:41.170
what you really wanted to solve
was the convex relaxation.

00:24:41.170 --> 00:24:44.350
So I won't go into
details on that

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:46.630
but that comes up
many, many times.

00:24:46.630 --> 00:24:48.760
And here's one in finance.

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:59.230
People would say, you what
is my 0.99 value at risk.

00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:04.300
That's how much money could
you lose in a 1% event.

00:25:04.300 --> 00:25:05.350
That's value at risk.

00:25:05.350 --> 00:25:06.910
By the way, it's
used universally

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:08.230
throughout finance banking.

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:12.620
It's written into
regulations, everything.

00:25:12.620 --> 00:25:14.980
So some people figured out
a very cool thing called

00:25:14.980 --> 00:25:16.295
Conditional Value Risk, CVaR.

00:25:16.295 --> 00:25:18.670
If you know, it doesn't-- and
you don't have to know what

00:25:18.670 --> 00:25:19.270
these are.

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:21.670
That's actually kind of
bad for value at risk.

00:25:21.670 --> 00:25:23.380
It's exactly the same thing.

00:25:23.380 --> 00:25:26.950
It's a convex surrogate
for value at risk.

00:25:26.950 --> 00:25:28.457
Everybody following this?

00:25:28.457 --> 00:25:30.790
It's convex and that means
we can do all sorts of stuff.

00:25:30.790 --> 00:25:31.940
We can analyze portfolio.

00:25:31.940 --> 00:25:35.730
We can construct portfolios
that have constraints on CVaR

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:36.730
and all sorts of things.

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:38.620
Everybody got it?

00:25:38.620 --> 00:25:41.560
10 years later, people
analyze it and find out

00:25:41.560 --> 00:25:46.060
that the value at risk is
what's called an incoherent risk

00:25:46.060 --> 00:25:49.690
measure and CVaR is not.

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:51.370
So they write down some axioms.

00:25:51.370 --> 00:25:53.000
I mean, again, this
is just the story.

00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:58.010
You write down some axioms that
a risk measure should have.

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:02.480
And what it means is
that value at risk,

00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:06.020
it's actually just a
bad measure of risk.

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:10.340
And too bad that it's what all
banks use, many hedge funds.

00:26:10.340 --> 00:26:13.340
It's written into
regulations so it

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:16.130
tells banks how much money
they have to have on hand

00:26:16.130 --> 00:26:19.680
to avoid a run on the bank and
all this kind of crazy stuff.

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:22.280
But anyway, so that's
a perfect example

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:25.130
where the convex thing
turned out to be actually

00:26:25.130 --> 00:26:26.390
what you really wanted to do.

00:26:26.390 --> 00:26:28.400
Not the one that
sounded the most natural

00:26:28.400 --> 00:26:29.750
in the first place.

00:26:29.750 --> 00:26:30.680
This is another one.

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:34.220
It turns out here's an
incredibly bad idea, is to say,

00:26:34.220 --> 00:26:35.600
I have a bunch of data.

00:26:35.600 --> 00:26:41.060
Please find me a separating
hyperplane that misclassifies

00:26:41.060 --> 00:26:42.050
as few as possible.

00:26:42.050 --> 00:26:43.820
But that sounds
completely reasonable.

00:26:43.820 --> 00:26:45.410
Anybody would say that.

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:46.293
It sounds reasonable.

00:26:46.293 --> 00:26:47.210
It turns out it's not.

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:49.043
Something like this is
actually much better.

00:26:49.043 --> 00:26:49.940
Yeah?

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:51.870
In this problem,
does it matter if we

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:57.105
choose plus/minus 1 as the--

00:26:57.105 --> 00:26:58.010
The margins?

00:26:58.010 --> 00:26:58.880
Yeah, exactly.

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:03.260
No, it is not going to
make any difference at all.

00:27:03.260 --> 00:27:05.950
You can make them 10
to the 6 if you want.

00:27:05.950 --> 00:27:08.450
And actually what would happen
is just everything would just

00:27:08.450 --> 00:27:10.880
scale because actually
if you look at it,

00:27:10.880 --> 00:27:12.380
everything is
homogeneous, including

00:27:12.380 --> 00:27:14.118
the objective this way.

00:27:14.118 --> 00:27:15.410
So everything would just scale.

00:27:15.410 --> 00:27:17.160
It wouldn't make any--
if you don't like--

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:20.660
if you don't like 1 as your
canonical positive number,

00:27:20.660 --> 00:27:21.770
choose another one.

00:27:21.770 --> 00:27:24.590
You could make an e just
to mess with people, or pi.

00:27:24.590 --> 00:27:28.710
You could make it
square root pi.

00:27:28.710 --> 00:27:30.300
That's not a bad
idea just to do that.

00:27:30.300 --> 00:27:33.450
Just so that somebody's
poking through your code later

00:27:33.450 --> 00:27:36.580
goes like, whoa, what is that?

00:27:36.580 --> 00:27:37.960
Yes?

00:27:37.960 --> 00:27:39.800
What do you mean by homogeneous?

00:27:39.800 --> 00:27:43.330
Oh, homogeneous
means that if you

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:47.860
multiply the arguments or
the variables, in this case,

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:52.090
by a positive number, then
that expression value goes up

00:27:52.090 --> 00:27:53.740
by the same number.

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:55.850
So a norm is a great example.

00:27:55.850 --> 00:27:58.180
An absolute value, a norm.

00:27:58.180 --> 00:28:03.460
And you can see here that the
variables are a, b, u, and v.

00:28:03.460 --> 00:28:05.710
And if I multiply
them all by 2, you

00:28:05.710 --> 00:28:07.600
tell me what happens
to the objective?

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:09.370
It goes up by 2x.

00:28:09.370 --> 00:28:12.890
So it's homogeneous.

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:14.840
Yeah, OK.

00:28:14.840 --> 00:28:19.210
Everybody got this idea?

00:28:19.210 --> 00:28:22.660
Well, you can actually do--

00:28:22.660 --> 00:28:24.670
I mean, I guess,
in machine learning

00:28:24.670 --> 00:28:26.200
you describe this
as kernel methods

00:28:26.200 --> 00:28:30.810
but we can just do this.

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:32.850
Suppose you want to
separate two points

00:28:32.850 --> 00:28:35.247
by some non-linear function.

00:28:35.247 --> 00:28:37.080
Now of course, there's
a gazillion functions

00:28:37.080 --> 00:28:39.630
that separate two sets of
points because I could actually

00:28:39.630 --> 00:28:42.060
define a function
that's positive

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:46.530
right around each of our xs,
negative around each of our ys.

00:28:46.530 --> 00:28:48.910
And then I don't even care
what it is anywhere else.

00:28:48.910 --> 00:28:49.720
It doesn't matter.

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:50.220
It's a half.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:50.940
I don't care.

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:53.430
Or 0, that doesn't
make any difference.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:55.140
That's silly.

00:28:55.140 --> 00:28:58.080
So I mean you want some
family of functions

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:00.420
or whatever to
regularize your choices.

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:03.120
And so you could do
things like this.

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:05.550
We can make a--

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:08.310
so f of z contains
these basis functions.

00:29:08.310 --> 00:29:12.300
And then theta is now a linear
combination of basis functions.

00:29:12.300 --> 00:29:14.160
One of them, by the
way, could be 1.

00:29:14.160 --> 00:29:17.430
That would be a very
common basis functions.

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:20.220
And then what you
want to do is you want

00:29:20.220 --> 00:29:21.580
to find these inequalities.

00:29:21.580 --> 00:29:25.050
Now this looks complicated
because in these things,

00:29:25.050 --> 00:29:27.450
there's sines, cosines,
hyperbolic cosine.

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:29.140
I mean, who knows
what's in those?

00:29:29.140 --> 00:29:30.880
There's some weird things.

00:29:30.880 --> 00:29:31.880
I have no idea.

00:29:31.880 --> 00:29:33.850
Maybe those are weird
radial basis functions.

00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:36.370
It doesn't matter.

00:29:36.370 --> 00:29:38.330
But it'd make you nervous.

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:39.830
But the point is that's data.

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:42.460
And so actually, these
are just vectors.

00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:43.720
They're just constant vectors.

00:29:43.720 --> 00:29:45.440
And it's exactly the same--

00:29:45.440 --> 00:29:47.680
It's exactly the same problem.

00:29:47.680 --> 00:29:51.720
So that makes sense?

00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:54.060
Maybe an even simpler
interpretation of this

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:57.270
is even better and really dumb.

00:29:57.270 --> 00:29:59.820
You think of this as--

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:02.430
I would think of this as
feature engineering, which

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:03.600
is like, thanks for your xs.

00:30:03.600 --> 00:30:04.860
Thanks for your ys.

00:30:04.860 --> 00:30:07.590
I'm going to put them
through some functions.

00:30:07.590 --> 00:30:10.560
Maybe I get a bigger vector.

00:30:10.560 --> 00:30:13.170
And you say now just do
support vector machine on that

00:30:13.170 --> 00:30:14.760
or separate them that way.

00:30:14.760 --> 00:30:16.620
That's another way to say it.

00:30:19.650 --> 00:30:22.320
So you can do crazy
stuff like you could say,

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:23.700
I want to do quadratic--

00:30:23.700 --> 00:30:25.710
I do want to do
quadratic discrimination.

00:30:25.710 --> 00:30:28.440
So to do that, you
set up inequalities

00:30:28.440 --> 00:30:29.700
that look like this and this.

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:31.800
And the variables
here are P, q, and r.

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:36.251
What kind of inequality is
this and this in P, q, and r?

00:30:40.170 --> 00:30:41.707
They're linear.

00:30:41.707 --> 00:30:43.290
Because the left
hand side is actually

00:30:43.290 --> 00:30:46.770
linear in the triple P, q, r.

00:30:46.770 --> 00:30:50.070
And you have to get used to this
because you see x transpose Px

00:30:50.070 --> 00:30:55.080
and you immediately
want to ask--

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:59.580
it is quadratic in xi but xi
is data so it's not a variable.

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:03.210
Your variable is P.
So that's what it--

00:31:03.210 --> 00:31:04.860
And you could do crazy stuff.

00:31:04.860 --> 00:31:07.230
Like you could
say, you know what?

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:12.270
I want to see if I can separate
some data by an ellipsoid.

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:13.950
And that would just be--

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:16.560
that would end up being an SDP.

00:31:16.560 --> 00:31:18.990
You'd have an
inequality constraint.

00:31:18.990 --> 00:31:21.030
You'd have a matrix
inequality constraint

00:31:21.030 --> 00:31:25.080
on P, something like that.

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:29.300
And this would be a
separation by an ellipsoid.

00:31:29.300 --> 00:31:32.625
So somebody want to
name a use for that?

00:31:35.400 --> 00:31:38.250
I give you a bunch of data.

00:31:38.250 --> 00:31:41.340
Let's say maybe these are
good outcomes or something.

00:31:41.340 --> 00:31:42.600
Maybe these are bad ones.

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:44.100
So anybody want to?

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:49.070
Location?

00:31:49.070 --> 00:31:49.880
What's that?

00:31:49.880 --> 00:31:50.780
Location.

00:31:50.780 --> 00:31:52.310
Yeah, I mean it could be--

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:55.580
Yeah, it could be that you
have some incredibly complex

00:31:55.580 --> 00:31:56.990
industrial process.

00:31:56.990 --> 00:31:59.720
You set your knobs various ways.

00:31:59.720 --> 00:32:01.520
And you run the process
and you get a bunch

00:32:01.520 --> 00:32:03.030
of data that looks like this.

00:32:03.030 --> 00:32:04.512
These are the ones that came in.

00:32:04.512 --> 00:32:06.470
These are the product
that came out up to spec.

00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:09.050
This is the one that failed.

00:32:09.050 --> 00:32:11.510
And so what this will
do is it'll give you

00:32:11.510 --> 00:32:13.310
an ellipsoid in the
original space that

00:32:13.310 --> 00:32:15.760
tells if you set your--

00:32:15.760 --> 00:32:18.080
this is where you
want to set your--

00:32:18.080 --> 00:32:20.450
this is how you want
to set those values.

00:32:20.450 --> 00:32:25.160
And you get an ellipsoidal safe
set or something like that.

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:27.140
Makes sense?

00:32:27.140 --> 00:32:31.430
And then you could do very
weird stuff like here,

00:32:31.430 --> 00:32:35.390
we've separated these
points by a quartic.

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:38.120
But anyway, once
you get the idea,

00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:41.820
you can do anything
you like with it.

00:32:41.820 --> 00:32:43.670
Although, you have
to admit it, looks--

00:32:43.670 --> 00:32:45.415
well, I'll try one for you.

00:32:45.415 --> 00:32:46.290
I give you a set of--

00:32:46.290 --> 00:32:48.810
I give you a set of points,
some xs and some ys.

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.060
And I say, I'd like
you to separate them

00:32:51.060 --> 00:32:54.467
by a minimum degree polynomial.

00:32:54.467 --> 00:32:56.050
Now I have to tell
you what the degree

00:32:56.050 --> 00:32:57.670
of a polynomial of x and y is.

00:32:57.670 --> 00:32:59.710
But let's say it's the maximum--

00:32:59.710 --> 00:33:08.440
a polynomial, sum it's a linear
combination of xi to the P i,

00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:12.560
y to the-- sorry, x to
the P i, y to the qi.

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:15.520
I'm going to take p
plus q as the degree.

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:18.860
So someone tell me, can
you solve that problem?

00:33:18.860 --> 00:33:20.320
And what kind of
a problem is it?

00:33:24.250 --> 00:33:28.990
How do you minimize the degree
of a polynomial discriminator

00:33:28.990 --> 00:33:29.770
for a given data?

00:33:34.520 --> 00:33:35.890
First of all, could you do it?

00:33:35.890 --> 00:33:36.390
Yeah?

00:33:36.390 --> 00:33:37.390
Iterate.

00:33:37.390 --> 00:33:38.200
Yeah.

00:33:38.200 --> 00:33:41.290
By iterate, you
mean try degree 1?

00:33:41.290 --> 00:33:43.570
Otherwise known as a hyperplane?

00:33:43.570 --> 00:33:47.020
It's a fail, then you say I'm
going to try quadratic, fail.

00:33:47.020 --> 00:33:49.800
Cubic, succeeds,
then you're done.

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:51.190
That was your algorithm?

00:33:51.190 --> 00:33:51.700
I like it.

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:53.860
Perfectly good.

00:33:53.860 --> 00:33:56.290
Is it anything else?

00:33:56.290 --> 00:33:58.420
We have a name for it.

00:33:58.420 --> 00:34:01.240
That's just quasi
convex optimization

00:34:01.240 --> 00:34:05.740
because the degree of a
polynomial is actually--

00:34:05.740 --> 00:34:07.840
I mean it's a silly one
but it's a quasi convex

00:34:07.840 --> 00:34:10.983
function because I list
out all the coefficients--

00:34:10.983 --> 00:34:12.400
Let's just do it
for one variable.

00:34:12.400 --> 00:34:14.320
I list the coefficients
of a polynomial.

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:19.870
And the degree is I look along
the vector for the largest

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:22.929
coefficient that's not 0.

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:25.059
That's actually quasi convex.

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:29.889
You just go on and
on with these things.

00:34:29.889 --> 00:34:32.389
You could probably write a paper
about some of these things.

00:34:32.389 --> 00:34:34.150
I mean, hilariously.

00:34:34.150 --> 00:34:35.275
Actually, almost certainly.

00:34:35.275 --> 00:34:37.275
It actually would probably
be better than a lot.

00:34:37.275 --> 00:34:38.949
Anyway, OK, I'll
stop right there.

00:34:41.679 --> 00:34:45.340
So our last topic is called
placement and facility

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:46.219
location.

00:34:46.219 --> 00:34:49.360
And there's a whole bunch of
problems that look like this.

00:34:49.360 --> 00:34:53.620
So it typically goes down in
like two or three dimensions

00:34:53.620 --> 00:34:56.800
right but there's cases where it
goes down in higher dimensions.

00:34:56.800 --> 00:34:59.020
And the way it
works typically is

00:34:59.020 --> 00:35:03.880
some of the positions are given
and the others are variables.

00:35:03.880 --> 00:35:05.500
And then for each
pair of points,

00:35:05.500 --> 00:35:10.120
I have a function that's
a function of the two

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:11.525
points, the two locations.

00:35:11.525 --> 00:35:13.150
I mean actually, in
many cases, they're

00:35:13.150 --> 00:35:16.190
0, meaning it doesn't
really matter.

00:35:16.190 --> 00:35:18.100
And then you just
want to minimize

00:35:18.100 --> 00:35:22.240
the sum of these
functions that are

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:24.430
functions of the two locations.

00:35:24.430 --> 00:35:28.940
And there are a lot
of interpretations.

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:31.780
So I mean the
famous one, I think

00:35:31.780 --> 00:35:35.300
it's called warehouse location
problem or something like that.

00:35:35.300 --> 00:35:37.760
And it basically
says what you do

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:40.370
is you have a bunch of
facilities i and j. fij

00:35:40.370 --> 00:35:44.000
is the transportation cost
between the two facilities.

00:35:44.000 --> 00:35:45.110
And that might grow.

00:35:45.110 --> 00:35:46.530
I don't know, it
might be linear.

00:35:46.530 --> 00:35:48.380
It might grow
quadratically or something.

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:49.130
It doesn't matter.

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:51.170
It's something.

00:35:51.170 --> 00:35:54.260
And then some of the
locations are fixed.

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:57.660
And then you want to choose
the locations of the other

00:35:57.660 --> 00:36:00.860
so that this total
cost is minimized,

00:36:00.860 --> 00:36:02.720
your total transportation cost.

00:36:02.720 --> 00:36:06.080
This is universal
in circuit design.

00:36:06.080 --> 00:36:10.790
So what happens there
is I don't know.

00:36:10.790 --> 00:36:13.910
You have any of the many chips
in my phone, and your phone,

00:36:13.910 --> 00:36:16.520
all your electronics
would have little cells.

00:36:16.520 --> 00:36:18.410
I mean, this goes
down at many levels

00:36:18.410 --> 00:36:20.010
but let's do it
at the gate level.

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:24.050
So you might have, I don't
know, 10 million gates.

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:27.980
Each gate is connected
to an order of magnitude

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:30.830
three or four others, roughly.

00:36:30.830 --> 00:36:34.280
I mean some have big fan
and some have big fan out.

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:37.550
But mostly, they're connected
to between one and three others.

00:36:37.550 --> 00:36:40.160
Or actually, I guess two
and whatever, four others,

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:41.630
something like that.

00:36:41.630 --> 00:36:43.130
Then what you want
to do is you want

00:36:43.130 --> 00:36:46.280
to choose where these
gates are going to go.

00:36:46.280 --> 00:36:48.530
And you want the ones that
are connected to each other

00:36:48.530 --> 00:36:49.670
to be nearby.

00:36:49.670 --> 00:36:56.330
And there, you would actually
take this thing, the cost.

00:36:56.330 --> 00:37:00.830
This is 0 if cell i
and cell j are not

00:37:00.830 --> 00:37:01.980
connected to each other.

00:37:01.980 --> 00:37:05.630
And this would simply be
something like the L1 norm

00:37:05.630 --> 00:37:07.100
if they are.

00:37:07.100 --> 00:37:10.190
The L1 norm because when
you run wires on a circuit,

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:12.350
you don't run like this.

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:15.050
You go this way and then you go
through a via to another layer

00:37:15.050 --> 00:37:17.570
and then you go that way,
or something like that.

00:37:17.570 --> 00:37:19.520
Everybody got that?

00:37:19.520 --> 00:37:22.040
So this is universally used.

00:37:25.370 --> 00:37:27.540
So that's an example.

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:30.270
We'll look at some
examples here.

00:37:30.270 --> 00:37:33.260
I mean, just a baby example
just so you can visualize it.

00:37:33.260 --> 00:37:36.230
And here, by the way,
the idea of designing

00:37:36.230 --> 00:37:39.950
penalties and functions
just comes up again.

00:37:39.950 --> 00:37:41.330
So here it is.

00:37:41.330 --> 00:37:44.030
I guess these ones on
the boundary we imagine

00:37:44.030 --> 00:37:45.230
are fixed points.

00:37:45.230 --> 00:37:47.270
In a circuit, those
would be something

00:37:47.270 --> 00:37:49.100
that you've already settled on.

00:37:49.100 --> 00:37:51.530
Or it could be
something like a pin.

00:37:51.530 --> 00:37:53.900
You'd say that's where
the external pin comes in.

00:37:53.900 --> 00:37:56.130
It's locked down,
that's where it is.

00:37:56.130 --> 00:37:57.950
That's where that comes from.

00:37:57.950 --> 00:38:01.400
And then you're
placing these things.

00:38:01.400 --> 00:38:04.820
So let's see what they are--
let's see if this makes sense.

00:38:04.820 --> 00:38:07.430
In this first case, so
what we're going to do

00:38:07.430 --> 00:38:09.080
is we're going to minimize.

00:38:09.080 --> 00:38:12.140
And the lines here
show you it's a graph

00:38:12.140 --> 00:38:16.010
and it tells you which things
are connected to which others.

00:38:16.010 --> 00:38:19.520
And what we do is on every one
of those, we take the distance,

00:38:19.520 --> 00:38:22.500
in this case, the Euclidean
distance between the two

00:38:22.500 --> 00:38:23.727
and we take a function of it.

00:38:23.727 --> 00:38:26.060
So if we literally just
minimize the Euclidean distance,

00:38:26.060 --> 00:38:28.400
we get this thing
here like this.

00:38:28.400 --> 00:38:31.730
So whatever this is, this is
the arrangement of those seven

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:35.030
or whatever black dots
in there in such a way

00:38:35.030 --> 00:38:39.020
that the total length of all
of those edges is minimized.

00:38:39.020 --> 00:38:43.110
Everybody got that?

00:38:43.110 --> 00:38:45.690
Now we go to--

00:38:45.690 --> 00:38:47.400
now we minimize the
sum of the squares.

00:38:47.400 --> 00:38:48.945
By the way, this is silly.

00:38:48.945 --> 00:38:51.095
I mean this is in a
linear algebra class

00:38:51.095 --> 00:38:52.470
because it's just
a least squares

00:38:52.470 --> 00:38:53.845
problem with
equality constraints

