00:47:46.710 --> 00:47:51.010
So that's the big picture.

00:47:51.010 --> 00:47:57.060
And so we'll start just by
talking about matrix structure

00:47:57.060 --> 00:48:00.252
and algorithm complexity.

00:48:00.252 --> 00:48:03.690
So suppose we want to solve
a set of linear equations,

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:05.610
like Ax equals b here.

00:48:05.610 --> 00:48:07.320
So we want to solve that.

00:48:07.320 --> 00:48:12.690
If A is for general methods,
it grows like n cubed.

00:48:12.690 --> 00:48:15.780
Oh, and I should mention
something about this.

00:48:15.780 --> 00:48:18.810
In theoretical computer
science, actually the number

00:48:18.810 --> 00:48:20.130
is not n cubed.

00:48:20.130 --> 00:48:24.200
Does anyone here know what
the current number is?

00:48:24.200 --> 00:48:26.471
Somebody here has got to know?

00:48:26.471 --> 00:48:28.523
Go ahead.

00:48:28.523 --> 00:48:29.690
By the way, I wouldn't know.

00:48:29.690 --> 00:48:32.060
So if you say the wrong
number, it's totally fine.

00:48:32.060 --> 00:48:33.350
I mean I know the range it is.

00:48:33.350 --> 00:48:34.913
What is it?

00:48:34.913 --> 00:48:36.080
I know it's between 2 and 3.

00:48:36.080 --> 00:48:37.840
It's like 2.4.

00:48:37.840 --> 00:48:38.490
Yeah, OK.

00:48:38.490 --> 00:48:40.400
It's around 2.4.

00:48:40.400 --> 00:48:41.840
Maybe 2.3, something.

00:48:41.840 --> 00:48:43.040
I have no idea.

00:48:43.040 --> 00:48:46.460
[INAUDIBLE] Microsoft
and Google because they

00:48:46.460 --> 00:48:49.350
