with a faster algorithm.

00:48:49.350 --> 00:48:49.850
Awesome.

00:48:49.850 --> 00:48:51.830
So it's what, 2.3 now?

00:48:51.830 --> 00:48:52.850
Somewhere around there.

00:48:52.850 --> 00:48:54.500
Yeah, OK, it's 2.3.

00:48:54.500 --> 00:48:56.150
So end of the 2.3.

00:48:56.150 --> 00:49:00.770
Now, I mean, that is
fascinating because it

00:49:00.770 --> 00:49:02.480
can't be less than n
squared because you

00:49:02.480 --> 00:49:05.450
got to look at-- you got to look
at all entries of the matrix.

00:49:05.450 --> 00:49:09.470
So you might imagine how could
you do this without n cubed.

00:49:09.470 --> 00:49:10.225
Anyway, you can.

00:49:10.225 --> 00:49:11.600
And it turns out
those algorithms

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:14.690
are utterly and completely
useless in practice.

00:49:14.690 --> 00:49:16.640
Just completely.

00:49:16.640 --> 00:49:19.640
I mean, look, maybe sometime
in the future they won't be.

00:49:19.640 --> 00:49:22.340
But right, now
they're just useless.

00:49:22.340 --> 00:49:25.190
Super interesting.

00:49:25.190 --> 00:49:27.770
And weirdly, the
methods people use

00:49:27.770 --> 00:49:30.510
to show that low
complexity in theory

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:32.610
are actually extremely
close to the methods people

00:49:32.610 --> 00:49:35.880
use to make solving these
things fast in practice.

00:49:35.880 --> 00:49:39.360
So I will say what
that is shortly.

00:49:39.360 --> 00:49:41.020
And that part is
not obvious at all.

00:49:41.020 --> 00:49:44.250
So the idea is super useful
but the actual results

00:49:44.250 --> 00:49:46.710
are utterly useless, those.

00:49:46.710 --> 00:49:48.790
Back to this.

00:49:48.790 --> 00:49:53.940
So what's cool is if A has
got some structure to it,

00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:56.400
then you can actually
solve this a lot faster.

00:49:56.400 --> 00:49:57.840
I mean here's a no-brainer.

00:49:57.840 --> 00:50:01.120
What if I told
you A is diagonal?

00:50:01.120 --> 00:50:03.430
Then you're like,
yeah, I know how

00:50:03.430 --> 00:50:06.100
to solve Ax equals b
when A is diagonal.

00:50:06.100 --> 00:50:09.880
But I said, what if was
a million by a million?

00:50:09.880 --> 00:50:14.260
You'd say, yeah, I can do
that in about 300 microseconds

00:50:14.260 --> 00:50:15.850
on my phone.

00:50:15.850 --> 00:50:16.652
Everybody see this?

00:50:16.652 --> 00:50:18.610
And you go, but how would
you store a million--

00:50:18.610 --> 00:50:20.185
and you go, well,
that's the point.

00:50:20.185 --> 00:50:22.060
I'm only going to store
the diagonal entries.

00:50:22.060 --> 00:50:23.977
I'm not going to store
the ones through zeros.

00:50:23.977 --> 00:50:25.960
I mean, that's an extreme case.

00:50:25.960 --> 00:50:28.150
I think most people
would get that.

00:50:28.150 --> 00:50:30.610
Profoundly stupid
case but there's

00:50:30.610 --> 00:50:35.140
going to be others where
it is not obvious at all.

00:50:35.140 --> 00:50:36.520
And we'll get to those.

00:50:39.140 --> 00:50:40.185
So here's how this works.

00:50:40.185 --> 00:50:41.060
And it's really cool.

00:50:41.060 --> 00:50:42.102
The history is very cool.

00:50:42.102 --> 00:50:45.980
It goes back to the
'60s, maybe even earlier.

00:50:45.980 --> 00:50:49.280
So a floating point
operation, it's

00:50:49.280 --> 00:50:52.020
like an addition of two floating
point numbers, subtraction,

00:50:52.020 --> 00:50:53.360
multiplication, or division.

00:50:53.360 --> 00:50:55.340
Truth is that's a
big old lie because I

00:50:55.340 --> 00:50:57.200
think a division takes
something eight times

00:50:57.200 --> 00:51:00.290
as long as an add or
something like that.

00:51:00.290 --> 00:51:02.810
But look, this is
just going to be--

00:51:02.810 --> 00:51:04.692
this is just for
order of magnitude.

00:51:04.692 --> 00:51:06.650
I'll also say the reasons
why all of this model

00:51:06.650 --> 00:51:07.700
is completely wrong.

00:51:07.700 --> 00:51:08.660
But it's fine.

00:51:08.660 --> 00:51:11.930
It's good enough for this.

00:51:11.930 --> 00:51:13.880
And some people got
weird in the '60s

00:51:13.880 --> 00:51:17.780
and they decided a floating
point operation was actually

00:51:17.780 --> 00:51:20.960
a multiply, plus an add,
plus some indexing work.

00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:22.330
And you're like, OK, whatever.

00:51:24.920 --> 00:51:27.590
So here's what you do is you
look at an algorithm that

00:51:27.590 --> 00:51:34.280
does stuff and you count
the number of flops as a--

00:51:34.280 --> 00:51:35.330
typically polynomial.

00:51:35.330 --> 00:51:37.760
There might be a log in
there which people just--

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:40.170
a log is just like-- you
just change it to a constant

00:51:40.170 --> 00:51:42.930
because that's what it
really is in practice.

00:51:42.930 --> 00:51:46.340
So you express it as
a polynomial function

00:51:46.340 --> 00:51:48.840
of the problem dimension and
you only keep the leading terms

00:51:48.840 --> 00:51:50.325
because this is very crude.

00:51:53.280 --> 00:51:59.280
So it is not remotely a good
predictor of computation time

00:51:59.280 --> 00:52:00.157
on modern computers.

00:52:00.157 --> 00:52:01.240
And this is just changing.

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:04.170
It's getting all very weird
and all that kind of stuff.

00:52:04.170 --> 00:52:06.395
I mean that should
be kind of obvious.

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:13.020
But it's useful as a rough
estimate of complexity.

00:52:13.020 --> 00:52:15.420
And a lot of people divide
all these things in their head

00:52:15.420 --> 00:52:18.460
into various groups like that.

00:52:18.460 --> 00:52:22.590
So the way, and this
is also historical,

00:52:22.590 --> 00:52:28.200
so vector-vector operations
are ones that take two vectors

00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:30.450
and you have an inner product.

00:52:30.450 --> 00:52:31.330
What does that take?

00:52:31.330 --> 00:52:33.870
Well, I have to multiply
xi by yi for each,

00:52:33.870 --> 00:52:35.940
so that's n multiplies.

00:52:35.940 --> 00:52:37.920
Then I have n minus
1 adds because I

00:52:37.920 --> 00:52:42.500
have to add the x1y1 y plus
x2y2 and blah, blah, blah.

00:52:42.500 --> 00:52:46.100
So basically, inner product,
people would just say n.

00:52:46.100 --> 00:52:48.320
I mean, the 2 is silly
so you just say n.

00:52:51.354 --> 00:52:53.270
The sum would be the same thing.

00:52:53.270 --> 00:52:55.600
It's just n flops.

00:52:55.600 --> 00:52:59.200
And by the way, these
have very cool names

00:52:59.200 --> 00:53:00.820
that's also a retro name.

00:53:00.820 --> 00:53:03.130
So if you know this,
you can impress people.

00:53:03.130 --> 00:53:09.460
So the first one, this is
called BLAS, B-L-A-S, level 1.

00:53:09.460 --> 00:53:11.530
Second is called BLAS level 2.

00:53:11.530 --> 00:53:13.240
And that's BLAS level 3.

00:53:13.240 --> 00:53:16.180
BLAS is Basic Linear
Algebra Subroutines.

00:53:16.180 --> 00:53:21.450
That's from the 1960s.

00:53:21.450 --> 00:53:23.635
But it's actually a
very good distinction

00:53:23.635 --> 00:53:24.510
to be thinking about.

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:30.900
And then by the way--
and some of this

00:53:30.900 --> 00:53:34.620
persists but gets really
crazy when you start

00:53:34.620 --> 00:53:37.490
looking at things like GPUs.

00:53:37.490 --> 00:53:40.580
And god help you if you
are looking at TPUs.

00:53:40.580 --> 00:53:46.760
But anyway, on GPUs, they're
crazy optimized for this.

00:53:46.760 --> 00:53:51.860
So there you can just get
like just insane speed.

00:53:51.860 --> 00:53:55.860
So anyway, I'll make some
mentions of that later.

00:53:55.860 --> 00:53:58.497
And these are things
you should know.

00:53:58.497 --> 00:54:00.830
If you're in the middle of--
if you're putting something

00:54:00.830 --> 00:54:03.530
together and you'd say that--

00:54:03.530 --> 00:54:07.380
you'd say, you can't multiply
those two matrices together.

00:54:07.380 --> 00:54:10.820
You should step back and say,
oh, that's BLAS level three.

00:54:10.820 --> 00:54:12.230
And I think our
friends at NVIDIA

00:54:12.230 --> 00:54:15.200
have a really good
idea about that.

00:54:15.200 --> 00:54:17.090
I think we can do
just fine, thank you.

00:54:20.930 --> 00:54:23.000
So you can also
look at the numbers

00:54:23.000 --> 00:54:27.890
here that BLAS level 1 are
linear in the single dimension.

00:54:27.890 --> 00:54:31.670
BLAS level 2, if you're
going to form Ax,

00:54:31.670 --> 00:54:34.460
matrix vector multiply,
that's going to be-- it's

00:54:34.460 --> 00:54:38.670
basically mn, the
product of the two.

00:54:38.670 --> 00:54:40.250
And let's see.

00:54:40.250 --> 00:54:41.270
Yeah, this is fine.

00:54:41.270 --> 00:54:45.260
I mean if things are sparse,
it gets very interesting.

00:54:45.260 --> 00:54:48.420
That's a structure
everyone should know about.

00:54:48.420 --> 00:54:51.230
So sparse, how do you
calculate the inner product

00:54:51.230 --> 00:54:53.770
of two sparse vectors?

00:54:53.770 --> 00:54:57.950
And roughly speaking,
how much does it cost?

00:55:02.300 --> 00:55:06.350
I'll give you two vectors,
each dimension 100 million.

00:55:06.350 --> 00:55:07.250
They're sparse.

00:55:07.250 --> 00:55:10.160
Each one has 100,000 entries.

00:55:10.160 --> 00:55:11.330
You tell me.

00:55:11.330 --> 00:55:13.220
I want the inner
product of the two.

00:55:13.220 --> 00:55:14.060
What does it take?

00:55:14.060 --> 00:55:14.930
How do you do it?

00:55:18.390 --> 00:55:21.200
I'll say I want to know
what the flop count is.

00:55:26.380 --> 00:55:26.880
Yeah?

00:55:26.880 --> 00:55:27.360
What do you do?

00:55:27.360 --> 00:55:27.860
Go ahead.

00:55:27.860 --> 00:55:30.400
You cycle through the
non-zero elements.

00:55:30.400 --> 00:55:30.900
Yeah.

00:55:30.900 --> 00:55:32.070
What you do is--

00:55:32.070 --> 00:55:34.650
the only time you ever
have to do anything

00:55:34.650 --> 00:55:40.428
is when there's an entry that's
non-zero in both vectors.

00:55:40.428 --> 00:55:43.920
So here's a big upper
bound on the number

00:55:43.920 --> 00:55:45.210
of flops it could take.

00:55:45.210 --> 00:55:48.600
It's the minimum of the
number of non-zeros.

00:55:48.600 --> 00:55:50.010
Everybody got that?

00:55:50.010 --> 00:55:50.970
Of the two.

00:55:50.970 --> 00:55:52.980
So that's an example.

00:55:52.980 --> 00:55:55.800
Matrix vector, same story.

00:55:55.800 --> 00:56:02.833
If A is sparse and I multiply by
a vector, that's going to be--

00:56:02.833 --> 00:56:05.250
you're going to have a whole
lot-- it's going to have to--

00:56:05.250 --> 00:56:06.990
it's going to depend on
capital N, the number

00:56:06.990 --> 00:56:07.920
of non-zero elements.

00:56:10.920 --> 00:56:12.780
And there's other structures.

00:56:12.780 --> 00:56:16.950
What if I gave you A as
a low rank factorization?

00:56:16.950 --> 00:56:19.426
So suppose I gave you A as a--

00:56:19.426 --> 00:56:22.860
A is a million by million
matrix which you can't store.

00:56:22.860 --> 00:56:25.980
But what if I gave it to you
as the product of a million

00:56:25.980 --> 00:56:29.910
by 20 matrix and a
20 by million matrix?

00:56:29.910 --> 00:56:34.180
Both of which I can
store on my phone.

00:56:34.180 --> 00:56:35.920
Then how do you multiply?

00:56:35.920 --> 00:56:40.420
How do you actually
form Ax in that case?

00:56:40.420 --> 00:56:43.450
Here would be the wrong way.

00:56:43.450 --> 00:56:45.355
This is the
absolutely wrong way.

00:56:48.340 --> 00:56:50.890
what did I multiply-- what
am I multiplying it by? x.

00:56:50.890 --> 00:56:56.620
This would be a very poor idea
because the first thing this

00:56:56.620 --> 00:56:58.930
would do is try to
attempt to allocate

00:56:58.930 --> 00:57:01.600
100 million 100 million matrix
and then it's game over.

00:57:01.600 --> 00:57:03.790
Everybody following this?

00:57:03.790 --> 00:57:05.800
But this is just silly.

00:57:05.800 --> 00:57:08.110
I mean, no one would do that.

00:57:08.110 --> 00:57:10.780
That's the right way.

00:57:10.780 --> 00:57:14.710
And then this one,
you just work out

00:57:14.710 --> 00:57:17.080
what it is and so on, and
then work out that one.

00:57:17.080 --> 00:57:19.960
So that's another very
common structure is low rank.

00:57:25.650 --> 00:57:28.230
That's good.

00:57:28.230 --> 00:57:29.230
This is the idea.

00:57:29.230 --> 00:57:32.370
So this is BLAS
level 1, 2, and 3.

00:57:32.370 --> 00:57:33.990
These are the different levels.

00:57:33.990 --> 00:57:36.390
By the way, there's some
very interesting things.

00:57:36.390 --> 00:57:39.480
The complexity of
matrix-matrix multiply, these

00:57:39.480 --> 00:57:40.470
are n by n matrix.

00:57:40.470 --> 00:57:44.230
Again, our friends in
theoretical computer science,

00:57:44.230 --> 00:57:46.110
it's like 2.35 or whatever.

00:57:46.110 --> 00:57:47.040
It's the same number.

00:57:47.040 --> 00:57:49.500
If Google figured out a
better number, that's awesome.

00:57:49.500 --> 00:57:50.610
Good for them.

00:57:50.610 --> 00:57:51.990
Everybody got that?

00:57:51.990 --> 00:57:54.030
And it's fine.

00:57:54.030 --> 00:57:56.160
Actually, I can
tell you how that

00:57:56.160 --> 00:57:59.810
works because the exact same
method is used in practice.

00:57:59.810 --> 00:58:04.290
So I'm going to show
you that just for fun.

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:05.640
Not the details.

00:58:05.640 --> 00:58:09.270
Actually maybe almost all the
details, but it doesn't matter.

00:58:09.270 --> 00:58:12.810
So if you multiply
two-- if I'm going

00:58:12.810 --> 00:58:15.030
to multiply two
matrices and this

00:58:15.030 --> 00:58:21.250
is A, B, C, D, E, F, G,
H, something like that.

00:58:21.250 --> 00:58:23.460
So what you do is
you just partition it

00:58:23.460 --> 00:58:26.290
into blocks like this.

00:58:26.290 --> 00:58:28.767
And you can work out what it is.

00:58:28.767 --> 00:58:30.100
I forget what these entries are.

00:58:30.100 --> 00:58:34.330
This is AE plus BG or
something goes here.

00:58:34.330 --> 00:58:37.090
And so then you have
a recursive method

00:58:37.090 --> 00:58:40.690
that says you just recurse
down and each of these

00:58:40.690 --> 00:58:42.040
is given by blocks as well.

00:58:42.040 --> 00:58:43.660
Everybody following this?

00:58:43.660 --> 00:58:45.137
And now comes the smart part.

00:58:45.137 --> 00:58:47.470
So now first we're going to
do some theoretical computer

00:58:47.470 --> 00:58:48.160
science.

00:58:48.160 --> 00:58:50.650
And that is somebody
figured out how

00:58:50.650 --> 00:58:55.450
to work this out with seven
matrix-matrix multiplies, not

00:58:55.450 --> 00:58:56.020
eight.

00:58:56.020 --> 00:58:58.150
Because your naive
method would use eight.

00:58:58.150 --> 00:58:59.740
They did it with seven.

00:58:59.740 --> 00:59:01.570
And now you just recurse down.

00:59:01.570 --> 00:59:04.090
And you're going to
get a number that--

00:59:04.090 --> 00:59:05.650
it's not going to be n cubed.

00:59:05.650 --> 00:59:08.560
It's going to be n to the
2.8 or something like that.

00:59:08.560 --> 00:59:11.380
Everybody following this?

00:59:11.380 --> 00:59:15.500
Now this is actually how it
works entirely in practice

00:59:15.500 --> 00:59:16.990
and this is your friend.

00:59:16.990 --> 00:59:19.930
By the way, normal people do
not need to know about this.

00:59:19.930 --> 00:59:21.800
But it's good for
you to know about it.

00:59:21.800 --> 00:59:28.780
So the way you multiply two
matrices, so it depends.

00:59:28.780 --> 00:59:30.820
Actually, we could even
do this-- just first

00:59:30.820 --> 00:59:32.950
let's do single thread.

00:59:32.950 --> 00:59:36.340
So single thread is
going to work like this.

00:59:36.340 --> 00:59:38.530
By the way, if you
multiply these out,

00:59:38.530 --> 00:59:41.620
you don't use the silly seven
multiplies versus eight.

00:59:41.620 --> 00:59:43.180
It's just eight multiplies.

00:59:43.180 --> 00:59:47.860
So if I say take your matrix,
chop it up into sub-blocks,

00:59:47.860 --> 00:59:50.320
chop those into sub-blocks,
and then just write

00:59:50.320 --> 00:59:52.150
a recursive thing, and go.

00:59:52.150 --> 00:59:55.070
Then someone says how many
flops do you end up doing?

00:59:55.070 --> 00:59:57.320
And the answer is n cubed.

00:59:57.320 --> 01:00:00.220
So it's the same as if you
just wrote a one line, a C

01:00:00.220 --> 01:00:03.220
program that said walk
across the first one

01:00:03.220 --> 01:00:04.890
and down the first
row and fill them.

01:00:04.890 --> 01:00:05.710
That kind of thing.

01:00:05.710 --> 01:00:07.002
Everybody following me on this?

01:00:07.002 --> 01:00:10.930
So you'd say, you haven't
changed the number of flops.

01:00:10.930 --> 01:00:13.990
How on Earth could
this be faster?

01:00:13.990 --> 01:00:16.510
Again, anyone want
to suggest something.

01:00:16.510 --> 01:00:19.272
You'd have to know a little
bit about computer architecture

01:00:19.272 --> 01:00:20.230
and how computers work.

01:00:20.230 --> 01:00:22.510
But I feel like all of us should
know a little bit about that.

01:00:22.510 --> 01:00:23.010
Yes?

01:00:23.010 --> 01:00:24.640
It's cache optimized.

01:00:24.640 --> 01:00:25.920
Yeah, that's exactly right.

01:00:25.920 --> 01:00:27.750
It's cache optimized.

01:00:27.750 --> 01:00:31.600
So what you do is--

01:00:31.600 --> 01:00:33.840
the lowest level in
the hierarchy are--

01:00:33.840 --> 01:00:36.360
I don't know, 16 by 16 matrices.

01:00:36.360 --> 01:00:37.170
Why?

01:00:37.170 --> 01:00:38.790
Because you can
multiply 16 by 16

01:00:38.790 --> 01:00:43.650
matrices directly at the
lowest level of cache.

01:00:43.650 --> 01:00:47.490
The next group up is
256 by 256 matrices.

01:00:47.490 --> 01:00:50.100
You can multiply two of those
using your level 2 cache.

01:00:50.100 --> 01:00:52.455
Everybody following this?

01:00:52.455 --> 01:00:56.230
And by the way, this
is from the 1960s.

01:00:56.230 --> 01:00:56.950
1970s?

01:00:56.950 --> 01:00:57.900
No, maybe '70s.

01:00:57.900 --> 01:00:59.760
Let's go with that.

01:00:59.760 --> 01:01:02.040
Everybody following this?

01:01:02.040 --> 01:01:05.790
So it's actually
completely stunning

01:01:05.790 --> 01:01:07.710
what happens when
you do this right.

01:01:07.710 --> 01:01:09.950
Oh, and by the way, the
same things go to a world

01:01:09.950 --> 01:01:11.200
where you have a lot of cores.

01:01:11.200 --> 01:01:14.400
So this could be a
GPU with 10,000 cores.

01:01:14.400 --> 01:01:18.360
And you say, oh, boy, oh,
is it good at multiplying?

01:01:18.360 --> 01:01:23.910
I can multiply 5,000 16
by 16 matrices by other 16

01:01:23.910 --> 01:01:27.180
by 16 matrices all
together in 2 microseconds.

01:01:27.180 --> 01:01:28.740
Everybody following that?

01:01:28.740 --> 01:01:30.792
So people who work
on these things

01:01:30.792 --> 01:01:32.250
know exactly what
those things are.

01:01:32.250 --> 01:01:33.730
They know exactly
what they can do.

01:01:33.730 --> 01:01:35.790
And so then it's the same thing.

01:01:35.790 --> 01:01:37.920
You'd chop these things
up into these things.

01:01:37.920 --> 01:01:40.710
And all of us are
utterly unaware of this,

01:01:40.710 --> 01:01:42.990
which is kind of cool.

01:01:42.990 --> 01:01:45.750
But we benefit like crazy.

01:01:45.750 --> 01:01:49.500
so everybody following this?

01:01:49.500 --> 01:01:51.013
So if you ever meet--

01:01:51.013 --> 01:01:53.430
I don't know if people even
do-- well there are people who

01:01:53.430 --> 01:01:54.990
do this in my department in CS.

01:01:54.990 --> 01:01:57.565
So anyway, if you meet one
of those people, just--

01:01:57.565 --> 01:01:59.190
remember I told you
if you meet someone

01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:01.080
who works on devices
and things like that,

01:02:01.080 --> 01:02:02.520
you should thank them?

01:02:02.520 --> 01:02:06.450
If you meet someone who works
on the lowest level stuff

01:02:06.450 --> 01:02:08.370
like this, thank them too.

01:02:08.370 --> 01:02:11.280
Because they're the ones
that allow the rest of us

01:02:11.280 --> 01:02:14.130
to use like NumPy and
SciPy, for that matter

01:02:14.130 --> 01:02:17.190
Torch and things like that
because it's just awesome.

01:02:17.190 --> 01:02:18.690
What they've done is amazing.

01:02:18.690 --> 01:02:20.260
And we're the beneficiaries.

01:02:23.460 --> 01:02:27.330
So if you didn't follow what
I was saying, that's OK,

01:02:27.330 --> 01:02:29.970
but you should know
about this, I think.

01:02:29.970 --> 01:02:35.460
Oh, let's talk about speed of
current compute and things.

01:02:35.460 --> 01:02:38.490
So you could actually
do things like--

01:02:38.490 --> 01:02:39.870
that you can do it yourself.

01:02:39.870 --> 01:02:43.260
And you can actually do
some of these calculations

01:02:43.260 --> 01:02:46.770
and make guesses about how
long something's going to take.

01:02:46.770 --> 01:02:49.650
Or you could actually say,
it did that calculation

01:02:49.650 --> 01:02:54.750
at this number of
flops per second.

01:02:54.750 --> 01:02:56.040
And by the way--

01:02:56.040 --> 01:02:58.278
so anyway, somebody
tell me what's a--

01:02:58.278 --> 01:02:59.070
here, I don't know.

01:02:59.070 --> 01:03:01.950
It's a four-year-old laptop.

01:03:01.950 --> 01:03:06.090
What will this do for, I don't
know, matrix vector multiplies

01:03:06.090 --> 01:03:07.737
and stuff like that?

01:03:07.737 --> 01:03:08.820
By the way, here's a hint.

01:03:08.820 --> 01:03:10.320
It's measured in
gigaflops, which is

01:03:10.320 --> 01:03:13.410
10 to the 9 flops per second.

01:03:13.410 --> 01:03:16.950
So what would be a speed number?

01:03:16.950 --> 01:03:18.710
I mean, you either
know this or you don't.

01:03:18.710 --> 01:03:21.210
But what would be a speed number
for a five-year-old laptop?

01:03:28.780 --> 01:03:30.370
How many billions
of flops speed?

01:03:30.370 --> 01:03:32.140
By the way, that's a lot of
floating point operations,

01:03:32.140 --> 01:03:33.070
just for the record.

01:03:33.070 --> 01:03:36.250
But how many of the-- how many
billions can you do in a second

01:03:36.250 --> 01:03:38.080
on like a--

01:03:38.080 --> 01:03:41.170
how about a newer computer?

01:03:41.170 --> 01:03:42.370
Anyone?

01:03:42.370 --> 01:03:44.060
Know the number?

01:03:44.060 --> 01:03:44.560
Range?

01:03:49.150 --> 01:03:51.850
It's between 1 and 10 for this.

01:03:51.850 --> 01:03:54.580
It could easily be 30 for this.

01:03:54.580 --> 01:03:55.480
Why?

01:03:55.480 --> 01:03:57.850
Because people who
know what they're doing

01:03:57.850 --> 01:04:01.540
have optimized matrix-matrix
multiply because people

01:04:01.540 --> 01:04:03.370
do it a lot.

01:04:03.370 --> 01:04:06.798
what would it be
on a simple GPU?

01:04:06.798 --> 01:04:08.590
Well, I have a GPU in
here for my graphics,

01:04:08.590 --> 01:04:10.900
so what is it there?

01:04:10.900 --> 01:04:13.670
Now we're talking 100 easily.

01:04:13.670 --> 01:04:15.040
That's a baby GPU.

01:04:15.040 --> 01:04:17.290
And on a real GPU?

01:04:17.290 --> 01:04:19.570
You start getting
towards the teraflops.

01:04:19.570 --> 01:04:21.970
And then it goes up from there.

01:04:21.970 --> 01:04:25.240
By the way, these are
just amazing numbers.

01:04:25.240 --> 01:04:27.250
And we're the beneficiaries.

01:04:27.250 --> 01:04:29.140
So I mean just as a
quick thing, how long

01:04:29.140 --> 01:04:34.010
does it take to multiply
two 1,000 by 1,000 matrices?

01:04:34.010 --> 01:04:35.450
Someone want to--

01:04:35.450 --> 01:04:37.070
I mean, look it's
order n cube plot.

01:04:37.070 --> 01:04:38.640
So that's 10 to the 9.

01:04:38.640 --> 01:04:39.890
So someone tell me the number?

01:04:42.420 --> 01:04:44.260
That's a gigaflop.

01:04:44.260 --> 01:04:46.350
That's a billion flops.

01:04:46.350 --> 01:04:47.603
How long does it take?

01:04:47.603 --> 01:04:49.020
I mean, I can just
do it right now

01:04:49.020 --> 01:04:51.420
but I'm not going to because
I know what would happen.

01:04:51.420 --> 01:04:53.370
But go ahead, somebody
what's the number?

01:04:53.370 --> 01:04:55.110
Does that take a minute?

01:04:55.110 --> 01:04:59.550
You'd say, wait, do you realize,
like multiplying 2,000 each

01:04:59.550 --> 01:05:00.150
of those--

01:05:00.150 --> 01:05:01.200
I'll make it sound crazy.

01:05:01.200 --> 01:05:03.270
You'd say, you have two arrays.

01:05:03.270 --> 01:05:06.270
Each of them have a
million numbers in them.

01:05:06.270 --> 01:05:07.380
Everybody following this?

01:05:07.380 --> 01:05:08.790
And then you have to
multiply them together

01:05:08.790 --> 01:05:10.680
and they all connect
with all of them?

01:05:10.680 --> 01:05:14.370
And you're going to do
a billion multiplies?

01:05:14.370 --> 01:05:15.370
That's crazy.

01:05:15.370 --> 01:05:16.440
Everybody following this?

01:05:16.440 --> 01:05:17.640
So how long does it take?

01:05:17.640 --> 01:05:18.840
1/100 second.

01:05:18.840 --> 01:05:19.600
What's that?

01:05:19.600 --> 01:05:21.220
1/100 second.

01:05:21.220 --> 01:05:22.200
That's about right.

01:05:22.200 --> 01:05:24.840
[CHUCKLES] So it's on the--

01:05:24.840 --> 01:05:27.570
Put it this way, it's
measured in milliseconds.

01:05:27.570 --> 01:05:31.560
So 1/100 would be
on a new computer.

01:05:31.560 --> 01:05:33.780
So this would be
a tenth of this.

01:05:33.780 --> 01:05:35.520
This would be 100
milliseconds here.

01:05:35.520 --> 01:05:41.370
And it's probably worth siting
down seriously pondering

01:05:41.370 --> 01:05:44.050
like how amazing that is.

01:05:44.050 --> 01:05:46.960
And there's a lot of
people to thank for that.

01:05:46.960 --> 01:05:51.203
So anyway, if whatever,
your roommates or neighbors

01:05:51.203 --> 01:05:53.620
or something do this kind of
stuff, you should thank them.

01:05:53.620 --> 01:05:55.870
So that sound good?

01:05:55.870 --> 01:05:59.200
Oh, and what about when
I'm on a super high-end GPU

01:05:59.200 --> 01:06:05.980
and I'm multiplying 20,000
by 20,000 dense matrices?

01:06:05.980 --> 01:06:08.320
You'd be terrified is the
answer, is what it is.

01:06:08.320 --> 01:06:11.573
It's unbelievably fast.

01:06:11.573 --> 01:06:13.240
But actually, that's
not the main point.

01:06:13.240 --> 01:06:15.130
We're going to be
talking about other stuff

01:06:15.130 --> 01:06:18.730
where you don't
need a supercomputer

01:06:18.730 --> 01:06:20.317
to achieve things.

01:06:20.317 --> 01:06:21.400
You just need to be smart.

01:06:21.400 --> 01:06:24.190
And that's going to be
the theme of the material

01:06:24.190 --> 01:06:27.132
we're going to look at.

01:06:27.132 --> 01:06:29.590
So we're going to start-- we're
going to build up real easy

01:06:29.590 --> 01:06:32.035
and just say, let's look
at some linear equations--

01:06:32.035 --> 01:06:34.010
when is it easy to
solve linear equations?

01:06:34.010 --> 01:06:35.530
Well, here's one time.

01:06:35.530 --> 01:06:37.360
If it's diagonal,
that's idiotic.

01:06:37.360 --> 01:06:40.480
It's order n.

01:06:40.480 --> 01:06:43.390
What about matrix-- what
if it's lower triangular

01:06:43.390 --> 01:06:44.902
and you want to solve it?

01:06:44.902 --> 01:06:46.360
So you want to
solve something that

01:06:46.360 --> 01:06:49.150
looks-- it's lower triangular.

01:06:49.150 --> 01:06:55.390
So it looks like A11, A21, A22.

01:06:55.390 --> 01:06:59.500
A31, A32, A33.

01:06:59.500 --> 01:07:01.600
I'm not going to draw the zeros.

01:07:01.600 --> 01:07:08.180
Times, let's say, x1,
x2, x3 equals b1, b2, b3.

01:07:08.180 --> 01:07:12.440
And you probably saw this even
in high school or something.

01:07:12.440 --> 01:07:14.250
So how do you solve this?

01:07:14.250 --> 01:07:16.620
Well, look, the first
equation is A11x1 equals B1.

01:07:16.620 --> 01:07:19.910
So x1 is B1 over A11.

01:07:19.910 --> 01:07:21.230
Now you know x1.

01:07:21.230 --> 01:07:24.320
The second equation is A21x1
but you know what that is.

01:07:24.320 --> 01:07:25.710
You already calculated it.

01:07:25.710 --> 01:07:30.740
So the second one
is A22x2 equals B2

01:07:30.740 --> 01:07:33.590
minus A21x1 which
you already know.

01:07:33.590 --> 01:07:35.630
So that method is
called-- that's called

01:07:35.630 --> 01:07:38.660
forward substitution
because you solve for x1,

01:07:38.660 --> 01:07:41.210
then x2, then x3, then x4.

01:07:41.210 --> 01:07:44.120
And whenever you
have one, you're

01:07:44.120 --> 01:07:46.700
going to substitute it
in future equations.

01:07:46.700 --> 01:07:48.560
Everybody got that?

01:07:48.560 --> 01:07:51.500
And when you work
out the complexity,

01:07:51.500 --> 01:07:54.125
that's going to be
actually n squared flops.

01:07:58.190 --> 01:07:59.450
So that's cool.

01:07:59.450 --> 01:08:02.720
By the way, it's
different from n cubed.

01:08:02.720 --> 01:08:05.900
In fact, there's a
factor of n in there.

01:08:05.900 --> 01:08:07.160
So you already know something.

01:08:07.160 --> 01:08:10.400
It says that if you had to
solve some triangular equations,

01:08:10.400 --> 01:08:14.240
you can do that really fast
compared to just solving

01:08:14.240 --> 01:08:16.520
non-triangular equations.

01:08:16.520 --> 01:08:18.140
And if it's upper
triangular, you

01:08:18.140 --> 01:08:20.330
do what's called
backward substitution.

01:08:20.330 --> 01:08:21.319
It's the same thing.

01:08:21.319 --> 01:08:25.640
In that case, you get xn first,
then xn minus 1, and so on.

01:08:25.640 --> 01:08:29.399
Oh, and by the way, this is
not how it actually works.

01:08:29.399 --> 01:08:30.500
This is blocked.

01:08:30.500 --> 01:08:32.870
That's how they actually do it.

01:08:32.870 --> 01:08:34.100
So there's little blocks.

01:08:34.100 --> 01:08:36.350
Again, we are blissfully
unaware of this.

01:08:36.350 --> 01:08:38.840
But when you solve
a triangular system,

01:08:38.840 --> 01:08:41.120
they don't do it line
by line like this.

01:08:41.120 --> 01:08:43.670
It's actually blocked.

01:08:43.670 --> 01:08:46.910
But we can pretend it looks
like this and the conclusion

01:08:46.910 --> 01:08:47.450
is the same.

01:08:50.510 --> 01:08:53.359
What if a matrix is orthogonal?

01:08:53.359 --> 01:08:59.350
Then it takes 2n squared
flops to compute A transpose--

01:08:59.350 --> 01:09:03.350
to compute, sorry, A
inverse B because A inverse

01:09:03.350 --> 01:09:05.569
is A transpose and then
it's matrix vector multiply.

01:09:05.569 --> 01:09:10.790
So actually, that's what it was.

01:09:10.790 --> 01:09:13.279
There's lots of other cases.

01:09:13.279 --> 01:09:14.930
If A could be--

01:09:14.930 --> 01:09:17.840
I mean, here's a very famous
structure for a matrix A.

01:09:17.840 --> 01:09:20.450
It's identity minus rank one.

01:09:20.450 --> 01:09:23.180
I think that's called maybe
a Householder reflection

01:09:23.180 --> 01:09:25.220
or somebody might know.

01:09:25.220 --> 01:09:27.920
Somebody in ICME or
something could tell me.

01:09:27.920 --> 01:09:28.850
I think that's right.

01:09:28.850 --> 01:09:31.430
That's called a Householder
reflection or something.

01:09:31.430 --> 01:09:34.189
Those, again, you
can solve in n flops

01:09:34.189 --> 01:09:36.649
because you just are
smart about how you do it.

01:09:36.649 --> 01:09:38.569
And the storage is also clear.

01:09:38.569 --> 01:09:42.050
You would store
u, not the matrix.

01:09:42.050 --> 01:09:44.060
A permutation matrix
is a good example.

01:09:44.060 --> 01:09:46.880
If you have a permutation
matrix and you

01:09:46.880 --> 01:09:48.930
want to know how
long does it take

01:09:48.930 --> 01:09:52.965
to solve a permutation
matrix times x equals B,

01:09:52.965 --> 01:09:56.310
you just unpermute them,
which is the transpose.

01:09:56.310 --> 01:09:57.990
And you actually don't
even do any flops

01:09:57.990 --> 01:10:00.240
because all you're doing is
you're just moving numbers

01:10:00.240 --> 01:10:01.020
around.

01:10:01.020 --> 01:10:05.310
So permutations is hilarious
because if you ask under a flop

01:10:05.310 --> 01:10:07.840
count analysis from
the 1960s or '70s,

01:10:07.840 --> 01:10:10.380
it's 0 flops because
you just move--

01:10:10.380 --> 01:10:12.780
it's actually like mem copies.

01:10:12.780 --> 01:10:15.510
You're just moving
numbers around.

01:10:15.510 --> 01:10:18.480
In fact, that can
take a lot of time

01:10:18.480 --> 01:10:21.690
if it's a vector that's 100
million high or something

01:10:21.690 --> 01:10:22.320
like that.

01:10:24.990 --> 01:10:27.130
So these are examples.

01:10:27.130 --> 01:10:29.460
And now we're going to
get to what the method is.

01:10:29.460 --> 01:10:31.560
And actually, it's
much more important

01:10:31.560 --> 01:10:34.770
to just understand the high
level idea than the low level

01:10:34.770 --> 01:10:38.360
details, except if you're
in the few people who

01:10:38.360 --> 01:10:41.300
actually work on these methods
so that the rest of us benefit.

01:10:41.300 --> 01:10:43.670
But the rest of us,
here's the method.

01:10:43.670 --> 01:10:45.590
It's actually very
simple and there's

01:10:45.590 --> 01:10:48.560
already immediately
weird things that you--

01:10:48.560 --> 01:10:50.120
I don't know, some
of you probably

01:10:50.120 --> 01:10:52.190
will leave today
knowing something

01:10:52.190 --> 01:10:54.320
you didn't know before
that's weird and shocking.

01:10:54.320 --> 01:10:56.720
So here it is.

01:10:56.720 --> 01:10:58.320
The general method
works like this.

01:10:58.320 --> 01:11:00.680
You take a matrix A. You
want to solve Ax equals b.

01:11:00.680 --> 01:11:02.680
You take the matrix A and
the first thing you do

01:11:02.680 --> 01:11:03.440
is you factor it.

01:11:03.440 --> 01:11:06.560
And factor it means you write
it out as a product of matrices.

01:11:06.560 --> 01:11:09.680
You know a bunch of these
factorizations probably.

01:11:09.680 --> 01:11:11.660
There's the QR factorization.

01:11:11.660 --> 01:11:15.350
There's the eigendecomposition,
singular value decomposition.

01:11:15.350 --> 01:11:18.770
You probably know a bunch
of these already anyway.

01:11:18.770 --> 01:11:22.730
So you factor it into a product
of matrices each of which

01:11:22.730 --> 01:11:24.860
is easy to invert.

01:11:24.860 --> 01:11:25.910
People say that.

01:11:25.910 --> 01:11:28.010
By the way, when they
say that, that's slang.

01:11:28.010 --> 01:11:31.010
It doesn't mean that anyone's
going to invert that matrix.

01:11:31.010 --> 01:11:32.840
When you say easy
to invert, it means

01:11:32.840 --> 01:11:36.235
it's easy to solve Ax equals b.

01:11:36.235 --> 01:11:37.860
That's what they
actually mean by that.

01:11:37.860 --> 01:11:41.760
But they just say easy to invert
even though no one is inverting

01:11:41.760 --> 01:11:44.470
any matrices.

01:11:44.470 --> 01:11:47.590
So this is really,
really quite dumb.

01:11:47.590 --> 01:11:50.830
It just says, well, if A is this
product, then the inverse of A

01:11:50.830 --> 01:11:53.410
is the product of the
inverses in reverse order.

01:11:53.410 --> 01:11:55.090
So I get this thing.

01:11:55.090 --> 01:11:57.560
And now I just put
parentheses the right way,

01:11:57.560 --> 01:12:00.310
which is to say I
first put a parentheses

01:12:00.310 --> 01:12:05.810
around the first one, A1
inverse b, and I calculate that.

01:12:05.810 --> 01:12:12.810
So actually, what you do is
you solve this to get x1.

01:12:12.810 --> 01:12:14.710
That's actually that
second expression.

01:12:14.710 --> 01:12:17.530
Then I have to multiply
by A2 inverse, which

01:12:17.530 --> 01:12:25.430
I do by simply calling the solve
method on A2 with right hand

01:12:25.430 --> 01:12:26.840
side x1.

01:12:26.840 --> 01:12:28.130
Everybody see that?

01:12:28.130 --> 01:12:32.428
So in fact, you solve this
by just a sequence of solves.

01:12:32.428 --> 01:12:34.970
You can even think of this in
a beautiful object-oriented way

01:12:34.970 --> 01:12:38.540
where each of these
things has a solve method.

01:12:38.540 --> 01:12:42.740
And solve literally
means you solve

01:12:42.740 --> 01:12:47.270
A, b, means it
returns A inverse b.

01:12:47.270 --> 01:12:48.943
And some of these
methods are idiotic.

01:12:48.943 --> 01:12:51.110
It would be diagonal, or a
permutation, or something

01:12:51.110 --> 01:12:51.650
like that.

01:12:51.650 --> 01:12:52.380
Doesn't matter.

01:12:52.380 --> 01:12:53.780
That's the idea.

01:12:53.780 --> 01:12:57.200
So you solve that by literally
just a sequence of solves.

01:12:57.200 --> 01:12:58.070
That's it.

01:13:01.250 --> 01:13:03.962
Now, usually the
way this works--

01:13:03.962 --> 01:13:06.170
There are exceptions which
we're going to talk about.

01:13:06.170 --> 01:13:08.780
But it works in a cool way.

01:13:08.780 --> 01:13:11.060
I mean in the simplest,
most generic cases,

01:13:11.060 --> 01:13:15.610
the factorization is
cost order n cubed.

01:13:15.610 --> 01:13:18.340
And then we'll see that
the solve actually is

01:13:18.340 --> 01:13:21.630
going to take order n squared.

01:13:21.630 --> 01:13:25.430
Now things get weird
because suppose I ask you

01:13:25.430 --> 01:13:30.860
how long does it take me to
solve one equation with 5,000

01:13:30.860 --> 01:13:31.470
variables.

01:13:31.470 --> 01:13:35.315
So 5,000 by 5,000 matrix
A, 5,000 vector b,

01:13:35.315 --> 01:13:37.890
and I want to compute whatever.

01:13:37.890 --> 01:13:44.840
I want to actually factor A.
Well, it's order of magnitude,

01:13:44.840 --> 01:13:45.740
it's under a second.

01:13:45.740 --> 01:13:47.657
I mean, it depends on
what you're doing it on.

01:13:47.657 --> 01:13:49.430
But it's not minutes.

01:13:49.430 --> 01:13:52.070
It's not milliseconds.

01:13:52.070 --> 01:13:54.090
Well, it'll be milliseconds
on some crazy things

01:13:54.090 --> 01:13:54.830
but that's fine.

01:13:54.830 --> 01:13:56.810
Everybody follow this?

01:13:56.810 --> 01:13:59.210
And now I'm going to ask you--

01:13:59.210 --> 01:14:00.400
let's say it's a second.

01:14:00.400 --> 01:14:01.560
We'll make it simple.

01:14:01.560 --> 01:14:04.070
Suppose I say, well, no,
now I want to do this.

01:14:04.070 --> 01:14:08.270
I want to actually solve
10 sets of equations

01:14:08.270 --> 01:14:13.070
with the same matrix but
different right hand sides.

01:14:13.070 --> 01:14:15.710
Well, the naive method
would take 10 seconds

01:14:15.710 --> 01:14:17.780
because you just have a
little loop or whatever,

01:14:17.780 --> 01:14:19.863
unless you had 10 cores
around and parallelized it

01:14:19.863 --> 01:14:20.510
but that's OK.

01:14:20.510 --> 01:14:21.380
Whatever, right?

01:14:21.380 --> 01:14:24.500
So it would take 10 seconds.

01:14:24.500 --> 01:14:25.585
Everybody following this?

01:14:25.585 --> 01:14:28.085
How long in fact does it take
if you know what you're doing?

01:14:33.800 --> 01:14:34.910
Let's review.

01:14:34.910 --> 01:14:40.020
So I want you to solve 5,000
equations, 5,000 variables.

01:14:40.020 --> 01:14:41.630
Takes a second.

01:14:41.630 --> 01:14:43.760
Now I'd say, OK,
thanks for doing that.

01:14:43.760 --> 01:14:46.970
I'd like to do it now for 10
sets with the same coefficient

01:14:46.970 --> 01:14:48.260
matrix.

01:14:48.260 --> 01:14:52.550
A normal person would
say, 10 seconds.

01:14:52.550 --> 01:14:54.140
I mean, are we right?

01:14:54.140 --> 01:14:56.180
And what is the actual number?

01:14:56.180 --> 01:14:58.370
It's 1 second, right?

01:14:58.370 --> 01:14:59.570
Why?

01:14:59.570 --> 01:15:04.850
Because if a 5,000 by 5,000
matrix took a second to factor,

01:15:04.850 --> 01:15:07.370
solve is going to be a factor
n smaller because that's n

01:15:07.370 --> 01:15:11.090
squared, not n cubed.

01:15:11.090 --> 01:15:15.680
So the answer, that works
out to actually microseconds.

01:15:15.680 --> 01:15:17.390
So the answer
would be, oh, yeah,

01:15:17.390 --> 01:15:21.200
it's about one second plus
maybe about 150 microseconds.

01:15:21.200 --> 01:15:22.850
Everybody following this?

01:15:25.700 --> 01:15:28.790
I mean, until you know
this, this is not obvious.

01:15:28.790 --> 01:15:31.580
So actually how many people
knew that basically you

01:15:31.580 --> 01:15:34.140
can solve 10 sets
of linear equations

01:15:34.140 --> 01:15:37.800
basically the exact same
cost of solving one?

01:15:37.800 --> 01:15:40.950
Some of you must have
known this or something.

01:15:40.950 --> 01:15:42.000
Everybody?

01:15:42.000 --> 01:15:44.465
OK.

01:15:44.465 --> 01:15:46.340
I'm not detecting that
you're that impressed.

01:15:46.340 --> 01:15:48.260
[CHUCKLES]

01:15:48.260 --> 01:15:51.200
Are you just like, who cares?

01:15:51.200 --> 01:15:53.630
Or I mean what?

01:15:53.630 --> 01:15:56.355
I mean, it's cool, right?

01:15:56.355 --> 01:15:57.230
I'll put it this way.

01:15:57.230 --> 01:15:57.990
I'm impressed.

01:15:57.990 --> 01:15:58.907
I think you should be.

01:15:58.907 --> 01:16:01.070
Honestly, I think
you should be too.

01:16:01.070 --> 01:16:02.960
By the way, this requires
that you actually

01:16:02.960 --> 01:16:06.560
know what you're doing
because if you actually just--

01:16:06.560 --> 01:16:11.290
if you say for right hand
side and right hand sides,

01:16:11.290 --> 01:16:14.330
solve, Ab, you're [INAUDIBLE].

01:16:14.330 --> 01:16:16.490
By the way, another
name for this

01:16:16.490 --> 01:16:20.040
is called factorization caching.

01:16:20.040 --> 01:16:23.730
So it means you factor once
and then do multiple solves.

01:16:26.430 --> 01:16:29.740
I think this makes sense.

01:16:29.740 --> 01:16:34.890
And the most common one
is the LU factorization.

01:16:34.890 --> 01:16:37.800
L is lower triangular,
U is upper.

01:16:37.800 --> 01:16:40.140
And it basically
says you can take

01:16:40.140 --> 01:16:42.240
a matrix A. If
it's non-singular,

01:16:42.240 --> 01:16:43.650
you can write it
as a permutation

01:16:43.650 --> 01:16:46.020
times a lower, times an
upper triangular matrix.

01:16:46.020 --> 01:16:49.390
The cost of that
is n cubed flops.

01:16:49.390 --> 01:16:51.180
And then this is how
you would solve it.

01:16:51.180 --> 01:16:52.860
It looks like this.

01:16:52.860 --> 01:16:57.240
So this one dominates.

01:16:57.240 --> 01:17:03.120
And then these things,
this is the solve method.

01:17:03.120 --> 01:17:06.570
This is where you're actually
walking through the three

01:17:06.570 --> 01:17:07.120
factors.

01:17:07.120 --> 01:17:08.940
That's why there's
three things here.

01:17:08.940 --> 01:17:12.210
Each of these is solving
essentially or computing the--

01:17:12.210 --> 01:17:15.840
it's working with the
inverse of 1 of the factors.

01:17:15.840 --> 01:17:19.180
And so this is what
you're doing here.

01:17:19.180 --> 01:17:21.760
And this whole thing
here is order n squared.

01:17:21.760 --> 01:17:25.090
That's order n cubed.

01:17:25.090 --> 01:17:28.180
And this comes up in
a whole lot of areas.

01:17:28.180 --> 01:17:29.320
So this is cool.

01:17:29.320 --> 01:17:34.990
And this is kind of the same
as the Gaussian elimination

01:17:34.990 --> 01:17:37.900
that you were almost
certainly tortured with either

01:17:37.900 --> 01:17:40.870
in high school or
undergraduate days.

01:17:40.870 --> 01:17:42.887
So it's basically this.

01:17:42.887 --> 01:17:44.470
By the way, when you
were taught that,

01:17:44.470 --> 01:17:46.090
did anyone say
that it has nothing

01:17:46.090 --> 01:17:49.120
to do whatsoever with how
people actually solve equations?

01:17:49.120 --> 01:17:50.260
Probably not.

01:17:50.260 --> 01:17:51.880
No.

01:17:51.880 --> 01:17:56.140
So why you would cause people
to actually do Gaussian?

01:17:56.140 --> 01:17:57.092
I mean, I don't know.

01:17:57.092 --> 01:17:59.050
It's like writing an
assembly language program.

01:17:59.050 --> 01:18:02.140
A person should do that
either zero or once.

01:18:02.140 --> 01:18:04.510
And absolutely no
more than that.

01:18:04.510 --> 01:18:07.480
And most normal people should
do it exactly zero times.

01:18:07.480 --> 01:18:09.220
But they should appreciate--

01:18:09.220 --> 01:18:11.170
Oh, sorry, we need
a very small number

01:18:11.170 --> 01:18:14.950
of people who are very weird
and like that to do that.

01:18:14.950 --> 01:18:16.720
That's all they do.

01:18:16.720 --> 01:18:18.220
So that the rest
of us can benefit.

01:18:18.220 --> 01:18:20.510
That's the way
this all works out.

01:18:24.460 --> 01:18:26.682
Anyway, so this is
basically the same as that.

01:18:26.682 --> 01:18:28.390
Except, it's not the
way it really works.

01:18:28.390 --> 01:18:29.800
Of course the way
it really works

01:18:29.800 --> 01:18:31.780
is all of this is the
same thing, except all

01:18:31.780 --> 01:18:33.460
these things are blocked.

01:18:33.460 --> 01:18:36.310
And if you have multiple
cores like on a GPU

01:18:36.310 --> 01:18:41.350
or just multiple cores, stuff
is being done at the same time.

01:18:41.350 --> 01:18:45.670
But other than that,
the idea is identical.

01:18:45.670 --> 01:18:50.760
So I think we'll quit here
and we'll continue next time.

